CinefexSearchData.register("text/35",{"issue":35,"date":"August 1988","pages":[["1","Cover","number 35 $5.50 Canada - $7.50"],["2","Closing pages",""],["3","Contents","cinetex CINEFEX 35 - August 1988. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $17; eight issues - $28: twelve issues - $40. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Riverside, California; color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright © 1988 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. ...the journal of cinematic illusions editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor Jody Duncan Shannon contributing editors Janine Pourroy Adam Eisenberg editorial assistant Ann Dredla Romancing the Rabbit.. What if cartoon characters were real performers who lived and breathed and worked on movie soundstages like any other actor? With the considerable might of Walt Disney Studios and Amblin Entertainment behind him, director Robert Zemeckis took that offbeat notion and from - a frenetic comedy adventure it concocted Who Framed Roger Rabbit combining live-action and animation with a degree of realism never before attempted. Mechanical effects by George Gibbs and Michael Lantieri enabled actor Bob Hoskins and others to interact convincingly with characters that had no on-set presence. These characters were later provided by some three hundred artisans working under animation director Richard Williams and were then seamlessly integrated into the - by the optical live-action - complete with shadows and highlights - wizards at Industrial Light and Magic. Article by Adam Eisenberg. 34 Willow whose flights of fancy have Filmmaking impresario George Lucas spawned the Star Wars trilogy and the adventures of Indiana Jones has focused on myth and magic for his latest excursion into the world of unbridled imagination. Manufacturing an earthbound environment every bit as wondrous and complete as Tatooine or Endor, Lucas and film director Ron Howard have produced Willow an epic sword-andsorcery adventure complete with fairy princesses, evil queens, firebreathing dragons, pesky brownies, talking animals and a diminutive hero determined to save an infant foundling from the forces of evil. Willow represents the most complete mustering of Lucasfilm effects talent since Return of the Jedi - employing effects animation, miniaturization techniques, stop- and go-motion, animatronics and computer generated imagery. Article by Jody Duncan Shannon. FRONT COVER - Roger Rabbit reacts hysterically in Who Framed Roger Rabbit. Jessica performs her nightclub act in Who Framed Roger Rabbit - BACK COVER."],["4","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","Romancing the Rabbit article by Adam Eisenberg"],["5","Who Framed Roger Rabbit",""],["6","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","6 4 CINEFEX 35"],["7","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","Since the earliest days of animation, filmmakers have tried to marry this uniquely cinematic form with live-action photography, With persistence of vision, pioneer Max Fleischer produced his classic 'Out of the Inkwell' series featuring an animated clown with a penchant for escaping the drawing board of his live-action artist. Somewhat later, Walt Disney reversed the process with his 'Alice in Cartoonland' series by inserting a live-action girl into animated adventures. As sound and color were introduced, sequences combining live-action and animation found their way into a number of feature films - perhaps most notably when Gene Kelly danced with Jerry the mouse in Anchors Aweigh and later when Julie Andrews and Dick Van Dyke entered a chalk painting world of singing barnyard animals and dancing penguins in Mary Poppins. Despite the popularity of these efforts, however, the illusion has never been completely successful - until now. Who Framed Roger Rabbit stars Bob Hoskins as Eddie Valiant, a none-too-prosperous private eye working the mean streets of Los Angeles in 1947 - a place and time where animated cartoon characters share an uneasy coexistence with live human beings in the real-world environment of high stakes moviemaking. When someone drops a safe on the very human head of gag entrepreneur Marvin Acme, the police are quick to finger an animated rabbit named Roger based on circumstantial evidence and some clandestine photographs Valiant had taken of the cartoon star's wife - a sizzling chanteuse named Jessica - playing patty-cake with the recently deceased. When the manic rabbit professes his innocence to Valiant, the reluctant gumshoe quickly finds himself entangled in the case. Searching for clues while fending off the sinister Judge Doom and his gang pletely animated Toontown district where cartoon characters live in ghetto-like isolation. A joint production of Walt Disney's Touchstone Pictures and Steven Spielberg's Amblin Entertainment, Who Framed Roger Rabbit is unquestionably one of the most ambitious and innovative fantasy films in years. Directed by Robert Zemeckis - whose previous hits include Back to the Future and Romancing the Stone - the high-energy mix of cartoon humor and film noir intrigue was freely adapted from the Gary K. Wolf novel, Who Censored Roger Rabbit?, by screenwriters Jeffrey Price and Peter Seaman. \"With Roger Rabbit,\" noted Zemeckis, \"we had a huge live-action period piece to create, a giant special effects film and a full-length animated feature all rolled into Who Framed Roger Rabbit photographs copyright © 1988 by Touchstone Pictures and Amblin Entertainment. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Bob Penn and Christine Loss. Effects unit still photography by Kerry Nordquist and Sean Casey. Special thanks to Hilary Clark, Howard Green, Jeff Walker and Steven Rogers. one. It was a very difficult project. But it was the perfect time for me to make this movie. I was too restless to do something like a small dramatic film, and I had gotten a taste of opticals on Back to the Future. On that film we had thirty shots and I didn't know how we were ever going to get through them. On this film there were more than a thousand! We had to contend with every special effects technique ever devised, except matte painting. You name an effect and we have it somewhere in the film. When we realized we were missing only one type, we thought maybe we should do a matte painting just for the hell of it - but in the end we decided against it.\" Determined to surround himself with the best possible talent, Zemeckis reunited with cinematographer Dean Cundey - who had photographed both Romancing the Stone and Back to the Future - and in quick succession enlisted the physical effects talents of George Gibbs and Michael Lantieri, the visual effects expertise of Ken Ralston and Industrial Light and Magic and the animation skills of Academy Award winner Richard Williams. Williams - perhaps best known for his work on the animated title sequences for Casino Royale and several of the Pink Panther films - was originally unenthused at the prospect of devoting two years of his life to a film that sought to combine live-action and animation. Essentially he considered the two forms incompatible. \"When I met Bob,\" Williams recalled, \"I started out by saying that I didn't like Mary Poppins. I love most of the Disney movies, but not Mary Poppins - and that was because I felt that while the animation itself was beautiful, it always looked like it was pasted on. You don't believe in the live-action because it has this pasted-on cartoon behind it, and you don't believe in the fantasy of the animation because you have this live-action intruding into it. You get the worst of both, and they vitiate each other. Bob said, 'I agree.\" \"I love Mary Poppinsas a movie,\" Zemeckis noted, \"but even as a kid I didn't like the scenes where the live-action and animation interact. Essentially they attempted to marry the two by making the live-action characters as flat as the cartoon characters - and they probably would have succeeded if there hadn't been so many shadows all around. In doing the scene, they planted Dick Van Dyke on a solid floor - painted to look like a cartoon floor - which enabled them to start the matte lines behind him. The problem was that they then blasted in light from all directions, creating fourteen different shadows for Dick Van Dyke when there were none at all for the cartoon characters. Even discounting the technical aspects, the animation in Mary Poppins and other such films has seldom been anything more than a gimmick - usually a dance number or something - where the movie itself comes to a stop, does its little interactive thing and then goes on from there. With Roger Rabbit the intent was entirely different.\" For Zemeckis, the key to suspending audience disbelief was in the very first sequence of the script. \"I think it was brilliant the way the script begins with a cartoon which transitions into a cartoon being filmed and then finally into the movie itself - with a human director In a bold attempt to breathe new life into an old form, director Robert Zemeckis embarked on a two-year odyssey to bring Who Framed Roger Rabbit to the screen. Combining liveaction with animation in a manner never before attempted, the film noir comedy - a $40 million collaboration between Walt Disney's Touchstone Pictures and Steven Spielberg's Amblin Entertainment - created not only a new cartoon star to rival those of the past but also established a forum that enabled most of those past giants to be featured in cameo. / Responsible for the design of Roger Rabbit - and for the crew of more than three hundred artisans and technicians tasked with bringing Roger and his cartoon costars to life - was interationally acclaimed animator Richard Williams. / Charged with overseeing the comprehensive plate photography and the marriage of live-action and animated images was visual effects supervisor Ken Ralston of Industrial Light and Magic. CINEFEX 35 • 7"],["8","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","In the Hollywood of 1947 - as set forth in the film - cartoon characters inhabit the real-world environment in a fragile coexistence with human beings. When gag entrepreneur Marvin Acme is found murdered, the prime suspect - a do-anything-fora-laugh cartoon star named Roger Rabbit - seeks help Valiant (Bob Hoskins) whose houses a bottle instead of a gun. To create the illusion of true interaction, most of the combining live-action and animation were abandoned traditional insistence that essential to the process. / Jessica Rabbit - a nightclub performer and wife to Roger (Stubby Kaye) during her sizzling floor show number at the Ink and Paint Club. from a once-crusading private eye named Eddie shoulder holster now established rules of - most notably the locked-off cameras were - tweaks Marvin Acme 8 - CINEFEX 35 stepping into the cartoon set and then the Toons walking off the set and into the three-dimensional world. I said, 'God, this is ingenious!' To do a dramatic movie with this kind of concept you have to have a scene that sets the concept - and this was perfect. I remember saying to myself when I read it: 'This has to be done in one shot and it has to be one of the most elaborate shots in the movie because this is what sets the universe.' To me it was absolutely critical. When I first met with Richard and he voiced his skepticism about the project, I explained to him what I wanted and then acted out that first shot for him. From then on, he was with us.\" \"We both agreed that the key to doing this movie was interaction,\" said Williams. \"The cartoons and the humans had to affect each other and the animation had to be lit in such a way that it would not look flat and pasted on. The question was, 'Could it be done right?\"\" Ironically, Williams had experimented with the notion of intermixing animation with live-action in a series of commercials for Fanta soft drinks that involved Disney cartoon characters. The commercials featured Mickey Mouse and Donald Duck darting between the legs of several real schoolchildren. Williams directed the spots and decided early on to move the camera as much as possible. \"I did a couple of shots where I violated the known technical formulas for working with live-action and animation. You're supposed to keep the camera locked down, for instance, but I had the camera three inches off the ground on a barrel with wheels and we ran through a bunch of kids playing football. Then we drew Donald and Mickey running between their legs and at the same time broke another rule that said you couldn't draw hard-edged cartoons and have them interact with blurred live-action. When I showed this to Bob, I said, 'What I found out was that I got a better result every time I broke a rule.\" Despite Williams' success, Zemeckis knew that creating such an effect for a short commercial was considerably different from basing an entire feature film on it. As a result, he decided to do a test - one that would determine if they could indeed make Roger Rabbit a reality. \"We said we weren't going to do the movie unless we did this test to see if we were even in the ballpark. So I sat down with Ken Ralston, Dean Cundey and Richard and we said, What we have to do in this one test is everything that we don't know for sure we can do.' The guy who was the most gung-ho was Richard. He would say: You can do anything! All we have to do is draw it.' Even Ken said: Well, I don't know. How are you going to do a crane drop in a perspective move?' And Richard would say, 'You draw it.\" The test was filmed at ILM with a human actor on a set that was designed like an obstacle course. The actor walked down a staircase, later to be followed by an animated Roger who was to trip down the stairs and crash into a pile of boxes rigged to fall. \"The rabbit was at the base of the stairs and the camera was at the top,\" Zemeckis explained, \"and as the rabbit got up from the boxes and looked around, the camera craned down from the top of the stairs and then started to move with him. We had the Toon walk behind things like a trash barrel with broken shards of glass to see how out-of-focus foreground objects would affect him. We had him walk in front of hot-lit windows and in and out of different colored rim lights. And then we did this push in at the end.\" The opening camera crane shot proved to be historic in that nobody had ever tried to do anything like it before. \"Supposedly, until our test, that had never been done in the history of the world. No one had ever done a crane drop with a live-action camera and planted an animated character firmly on the ground. Everyone all through time said it was impossible to do.\" Once the live-action was shot, the plate was sent to London where Williams set about adding Roger Rabbit to it. A design for the character had been established during numerous early meetings with Spielberg, Zemeckis and producers Frank Marshall and Robert Watts. \"We had a whole bunch of rabbit drawings,\" Williams recollected. \"Steven Spielberg felt the rabbit should be very sympathetic. In fact, when I was trying to work on the mouth, he said, 'You know, my favorite cartoon rabbit mouth ever is Thumper. ' Then Bob would point to this Tex Avery mouse with a cashew-shaped head and suggest we try to get some of that in. So I drew a rabbit on top of that mouse, and then someone else came in and said, 'That rabbit looks like a mouse.' So we had to find a balance. I ended up drawing Disney rabbits, Warner rabbits, M-G-M rabbits and different colored rabbits. I guess I was the one for the job because I know all the different rabbits.\" Zemeckis and Williams agreed that Roger had to look somewhat familiar so that audiences would feel they had seen him before. Yet he also had to be unique, with a fully developed design and personality. In actuality, cartoon characters like Bugs Bunny and Daffy Duck were developed over a period of time in a progression of films, but Williams had to make the leap to full development in one jump. \"Friz Freleng once said: These characters are designs. They're not rabbits or mice - they're designs.' So I tried to do it as if I were a 1947 designer and design Roger as if he were being made then. It was Bob's secretary who suggested that Roger be white, which I thought was a pretty good idea because none of the other rabbits of the period were white and Roger had to be different. As soon as I got the white rabbit with a cashew-shaped head, I gave him spoon-shaped ears, a triangular body like a European clown and a thatch of red hair like Goofy has. And that was it. Goofy had overalls at the time, so I put Roger in red overalls. Then I thought, if he's a white rabbit with red overalls I can give him a blue tie and blue eyes and he'll look like the American flag - and subliminally everybody is going to like it. He has pink in the ears and the feet, but basically he's the American flag.\" For the ILM test, Williams took his early designs of Roger and animated the character through the booby-trapped plate photography. He also created several tone mattes and additional shadows. Once the animation was completed, he shipped it off immediately to ILM so it could be combined optically with the"],["9","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","live-action. Then he waited. \"The next thing I heard was about two weeks later. Zemeckis rang up and he said: I've never seen anything like it! This is itl You won't believe itl' And I said, What do you mean?' He said: 'The test, the test! It works! Spielberg's seen it in New York and he loves it. He said he's only seen film history twice in his life. The first time was with the tests that George Lucas did for Star Wars - and now he's seen it again with this rabbit.\" \"It was pretty impressive,\" Zemeckis enthused. \"We were all really blown away. I said, 'Wow, we're really going to pull this off. It was breathtaking. Once the guys at Disney saw the test, that was it. Green light. Make the movie. It had to be done. Animation is the heritage of the Walt Disney company - and we were about to embark on animation for the '90s.\" What the test proved most of all was that Zemeckis could realize his dream of shooting Roger Rabbit like a normal live-action film in which the Toons would have to adhere to the spatial realities of our three-dimensional universe. If the camera moved in a given shot, then the animation would have to change perspective and constantly adjust to the changing point of view. If a light in the scene was swinging, causing shadows to sweep back and forth across the human actors, then the Toon characters would have to be lit in exactly the same way. Again, the key was interaction - having the Toons not only move physical props, but also interact with the real environment around them.\" Making all of this happen would involve three basic stages. First, the live-action would have to be filmed with Bob Hoskins and other cast members working alone in a maelstrom of complex physical effects. Then Williams and his team of animators would create the Toon characters - some famous, some not - to share these scenes with the humans. Finally the live-action and animation would be sandwiched together at ILM under the watchful eye of optical supervisor Edward Jones. None of the steps was easy. The first stage of the process began in Los Angeles where several key exteriors were photographed. The most complex of these involved a furious chase between an animated taxi with Eddie Valiant at the wheel and a real patrol vehicle being driven by the animated weasels. In earlier screen efforts, such a sequence might have been done by filming Hoskins on a bluescreen stage and then matting him into a background plate shot on location. For Roger Rabbit, however, Zemeckis wanted first generation imagery of Hoskins actually on the street riding in a car that could later be concealed by the taxicab animation. Engaged to produce the vehicle - as well as all the other physical effects required for the Los Angeles shoot - was special effects supervisor Michael Lantieri. \"During our first meeting, Bob Zemeckis said, We want to build a vehicle that we call Benny the Car.' He said it had to be able to carry a person and have a seat that moved back and forth. It also had to stretch, go forward and in reverse, travel at least forty miles per hour, be able to corner tighter than a Porsche and do a full 360degree turn - all with the actor in it. That was my introduction CINEFEX 35 • 9"],["10","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","10 - CINEFEX 35"],["11","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","to the film.\" With only a limited time to prepare, Lantieri spent several days looking for a vehicle he could modify to fit the bill. In the end, two were built - both made from Honda four-wheeldrive, off-road vehicles. \"I brought in Robert Spurlock and Clay Pinney to do the primary engineering on the cars. One of them was articulated so that the front would fly out like a cartoon car when it was taking off. The front end would stretch out three feet and then the rest of the car would suddenly slide forward to catch up - like a big rubber band. We had to cut the frame of the Honda in half and put in air rams to do that.\" \"The driver's seat also had air rams,\" assistant Don Elliot added, \"and was fully gimbaled four ways so it could throw Hoskins back and forth as he went around turns. He would sit there holding a fake steering wheel while the actual stunt driver was slung underneath him - his legs were under Hoskins' seat - and the only way he could see was to look around the side. His head was where the spare tire on the animated Benny would be, so if he leaned back even three inches he would have been outside the animation and we would have had a problem. Everything had to be measured exactly so that it would fit inside the parameters of the animation.\" In contrast to the animated taxi being driven by a live performer, the patrol van in pursuit required a real vehicle driven by animated weasels. \"The steering and the shifting and the brakes all had to be remoted to the back of the truck,\" Lantieri explained, \"so a man could sit back there and drive the vehicle with no one in the front cab. From the rear we could also open and close all the doors, turn the lights on and off and drive up and down through traffic. The driver in back was looking through a small slot about two inches by four inches that was cut into the rear of the cab. That worked quite well, though it proved more difficult at night and once we did accidentally skid into a fire hydrant.\" Considering the nature of your average cartoon weasel, operating the truck was sometimes a bit chaotic. \"In one shot, the truck pulls up real quick, the cab door opens and the weasels jump out,\" Elliot recalled. \"Then the weasels run to the back, open up the back doors, jump into the rear of the van and close the doors. Then the back doors open again, one of the weasels climbs out, gets back into the cab and drives away. When we did the shot, Michael and I sat in the back. We were dressed in black and hidden behind a black curtain so that when the back door opened we would not be seen by the camera. Then we went through about thirty different actions to accomplish what was needed for the scene. It was hot and we were laughing, uncertain if we were getting everything with all the right timings. When the back doors closed for the final time, we sat back and awaited our fate. Someone opened the door and the whole crew was applauding. They said the scene was just hilarious - and that was before they had drawn in the characters.\" Lantieri also supervised the construction of two Pacific and Electric 'Red Cars' reminiscent of the actual trolley cars that once operated throughout Los Angeles. 'We had the streetcars made by Dean Jeffries - he built two of them on bus frames. On location, we put phony track down on the street, hung cable overhead and put an arm on top of the cars with a small sparking device so it would look like the cars were actually running off the cable. In reality, they were powered by bus motors and we could drive them and turn them around. The trolley arms were never meant to actually touch the cables, but sometimes they would get caught and we would have to move pretty fast. The cables were strung on nearby telephone poles, and if they got snagged they would start bending off the poles.\" The streetcars were a key ingredient in recreating the Los Angeles of the period. In addition, storefronts along several blocks of downtown Hope Street were refitted with a look from yesteryear. For animator Williams, the setting echoed a period from his own past when he had gotten his first taste of being an animator. \"I was really taken with animation when I was five and first saw Snow White. My parents were illustrators and my mother had friends at the Disney Studios. A story artist sent me a lot of stuff, and it marked my life as a kid. When I was fifteen and went for the summer to L.A., I stayed at the YMCA on South Hope Street and tried to get into Disney. Finally I got in, and they did stories on me that they still have in their archives. For me it was the greatest thing ever. That was 1948. The irony is that this film is set in 1947, and when they started filming the exteriors on South Hope Street it looked exactly the way I remembered it as a kid.\" \"We shot for three weeks,\" Lantieri recalled, \"and every day we were required to provide some effect. Every shot - no rest. That was because the way to tie the cartoon characters into the real world was by having the cartoons move actual physical props during the shots. And whenever they had to do that, we were behind them. If one of the animated characters had to step into a mud puddle, it was up to us to create a small splash. If they walked through some bushes, we had to move the bushes. If they made footprints in dirt, we had to create those footprints. We had to do all sorts of little effects.\" \"On Roger Rabbit,\" noted coproducer Robert Watts, \"about eighty percent of the movie we were shooting with only half the cast present. Therefore all the props that were being moved by the cartoon characters had to be physically moved on the set. Thus it became like shooting a giant 'Invisible Man' movie - and on that level, it was very difficult.\" To aid Lantieri during the shoot, full-size rubber dummies of Roger and the weasels were made by George Gibbs in London. \"Richard Williams drew what they would look like,\" said Lantieri, \"and then George made them into foam characters and sent them to us so we could use them for anything we built. If we had to build a place for Roger to sit or an arm for a weasel to hold a gun, we would measure off the dummies to make sure we had the right size. We didn't want to risk showing up with a mechanical arm that had a bolt sticking out somewhere that couldn't be covered by animation. Actually, we got real lucky. We never had to rebuild anything once we brought it to the set. Sometimes we had to On the Ink and Paint Club set constructed at the Cannon Elstree Studios in London, actress Betsy Brantley steps through the choreography of Jessica's song. Her cartoon counterpart would later be drawn over her, though Jessica's impossibly waspwaisted figure required a separate motion control pass so that ILM could splitscreen out the lower twothirds of Brantley's body. / Vistavision was employed almost exclusively during principal photography since nearly every scene in the film would require some degree of animation or effects enhancement. Cinematographer Dean Cundey, Bob Hoskins, Robert Zemeckis and Ken Ralston gather around a video monitor between takes to evaluate performance and technical sufficiency. / Video technician lan Kelly was tasked with recording every take for instant playback and reference. CINEFEX 35 • 11"],["12","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","Since Roger and his cartoon mechanisms and rigs had to be devised to suggest their physical presence. Moreover, these rigs had to be either invisible to the camera or sized and positioned so that the animated characters - added later - would conceal them. In England, physical effects supervisor George Gibbs was responsible for such effects. For a scene in which Roger emerges from Valiant's kitchen sink and water, Gibbs rigged a spout to pop up out of the sink, spray a stream of water and Approved live-action plates photographic prints - one for each frame in the shot - so that the animators could mattes and composited the costars had to interact directly with real-world people and props, an extensive array of spits out a mouthful of then submerge again. were then transferred to align their character drawings with precision. Once the animation was completed, the optical department at ILM added tone mattes and shadow multitude of elements. 12 * CINEFEX 35 apply things differently - bend a few rods or trim a few wires - but nothing more than that.\" The lifesize rubber dummies were also useful in rehearsing with the actors and in providing a reference for the animators. \"After I got a printed take,\" said Zemeckis, \"I would walk the dummy through the scene and shoot it again, just for reference, with all the human actors repeating what they had done in the printed take. That was important to show where the cartoon character would be in the shot and the size relationships and how the perspective would change as it moved around. Also it was important as a lighting reference to show exactly where shadows were being cast and how light was hitting a threedimensional version of the character. It was really invaluable for the animators and we did that on every single take.\" Lantieri's most complex shot was one that appears early in the film when a bewildered Eddie Valiant strolls through the bustling Maroon Cartoon Studio past a wide assortment of Toon actors and technicians. \"Filming that opening shot was probably one of the biggest days we had because everywhere you see a cartoon lurking we had to do something. There are dancing brooms using real brooms to sweep a soundstage, a pelican riding a real bicycle, a hippo that sits down on a bench and catapults a man up into the air and a crate filled with animated musical instruments that falls off a truck. We had to do about twelve different effects, and all of them had to happen at virtually the same time because it was one continuous shot.\" Some effects in the sequence were relatively easy, others more complicated. The real pushbrooms, for instance, were animated via concealed rods - with animated brooms later drawn in to appear as though they were guiding them. Other relatively simple effects included a crate that was equipped to open from the inside - allowing the Toon instruments to burst free - and a bench that was rigged to flip up when the hippo sat down. A more complicated effect was needed to enable the Toon pelican to ride a real bike. For this, Lantieri and his crew had to effectively create a riderless bicycle - one that could glide out of a soundstage and pedal across the street. \"We pushed a bicycle back and forth in a parking lot for hours,\" Elliot explained. \"We thought of making a gyro and mounting it on the bike, but we didn't have two months to invest in making a gyro that would fit into a bicycle basket. Who knows how the idea popped into my head, but I thought of filling the tires with water. So we did that. Not only did the water stabilize the bike and keep it balanced, but the weight of it also kept the tires moving forward, causing the pedals to rotate. You could get the bike running and it would go straight.\" At the actual shooting location - the Ren Mar Studios in Hollywood - the bike was propelled along and steered by a cable to maintain its course. During preproduction, when it became apparent to the filmmakers that nearly every scene in Roger Rabbit would require some sort of optical effect, a decision was made to shoot the film almost entirely in Vistavision for maximum image quality. Such a thing had not been done since the 1950s and no stateof-the-art Vistavision cameras existed that could effectively meet the task. Consequently, ILM decided on very short notice to build the world's first silent reflex Vistavision cameras - the design and manufacture of which fell primarily to camera enJineers Mike Bolles and Greg Beaumonte. 'The cameras were not ready when we filmed in Los Angeles,\" said Zemeckis, \"so we had to use some old butterfly Vistavision effects cameras. To baffle the noise they make, ILM had to throw together some blimps that were built in a week just for the few dialogue scenes we had to do. These blimps had no optical glass in front of them and we were worried about reflections and glares, but they worked out fine. Instead of Vistavision, we used Super 35 in some of the car chase scenes because we knew we wouldn't be able to move fast enough with the old butterfly cameras. Besides, there were not enough of them around to shoot second unit down the street while I was working with my actors. Also we had motorcycle mounts and things for the chase that were too small for our butterfly cameras anyway. So Ken suggested we go with a more compact Super 35 camera for some of the real quick cuts.\" After the location work in Los Angeles, the production unit moved on to London where interiors were slated to be shot at the Cannon Elstree Studios. The new Vistavision cameras showed up just in time to be used. \"The cameras were ready one week before we started shooting,\" Watts recalled. \"They had film through them for the very first time - which was slightly nerveracking because usually a newly-designed camera is going to have some teething problems. But in this particular instance, someone was looking out for us because we never had any problems with either of those two cameras.\" At Elstree, the human actors shared center stage with effects orchestrated by George Gibbs - a twenty-five-year veteran with an Oscar for his work on Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom and British Academy Awards for Indiana Jones and Brazil. While most of Lantieri's effects had been large-scale - like mock taxis and trolley cars - Gibbs had to create more intimate effects for the cartoon characters as they moved about the various interior sets. \"The difficult thing about the effects for Roger Rabbit was making objects move that were later going to be cartooned over. For example, there were times when Roger Rabbit would have to pick up a real handkerchief and blow his nose or pick up a real glass and have a drink. Or there would be a scene in the Terminal Bar where he's dancing around and picking up plates and smashing them on his head - and they all had to be real plates. Then we also had Baby Herman who smokes a real cigar. For both Roger and Baby Herman we built robotic arms worked by little servo motors that are very highly geared to give them a lot of torque. The Baby Herman arm we claim to be the smallest robotic arm in the world - it was no bigger than the size of a human baby.\" The robotic arms were actually manipulated by human operators wearing a remote slave armature. Small encoders on"],["13","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","this armature relayed exact positions of the human arm to the robotic arm, enabling the robotic arm to duplicate the human moves. \"Carlo Rambaldi and a lot of other effects men work only with cables, but we do everything with servos. I find it cuts down on manpower and it's a lot cheaper. When you do miniature arms, the difficult thing is getting the gearing strong enough and small enough. But when you're doing it on big stuff, you don t have any problems because it's very strong - much ree time stronger than with cables that are snapping all the time. Of course, it doesn't work for everything, We use what we call 'horses for courses' - you have a special type of race horse for a special type of race course. You get some horses that are good at running on hard ground and some horses that are good running on soft ground. Sometimes we used armatures for the weasels and sometimes we used just sticks and things - sometimes we used wires. It depended on what we were shooting at the time. That went for Roger also.\" To facilitate shots employing wires and puppetry, Gibbs suggested that all of the sets be built at least six feet above the floor so that human operators and puppeteers could work from below if necessary. Throughout the shoot, Ralston and Gibbs conferred to make certain the physical effects would fit within the parameters of the animation. \"In many scenes,\" said Ralston, \"Roger would be walking around the set, grabbing stuff here and there. In one shot in the Terminal Bar, he was nervously grabbing things out of a toolbox and flinging them across the room. He even grabs a hammer at one point. These kinds of effects could be accomplished much more simply without the robotic arm. The hammer, for instance, was suspended on wires that we were able to later hide behind Roger.\" In another scene, the weasels burst into Valiant's office and living quarters in search of the slap-happy hare who has clownishly handcuffed himself to his benefactor's wrist. Quick on his feet, the detective dunks Roger into a kitchen sink filled with dirty socks and then proceeds to wash his laundry while the weasels search the apartment. During the scene, the cocky lead weasel named Smart Ass pulls a real gun on Valiant. \"We had a robot arm come up from behind where the weasel would be,\" Ralston explained, \"giving the impression that the weasel reached down to get the gun. Then Smart Ass lowers the gun and splashes dishwater on Valiant. For that, the dishwater was rigged to splash. Then, after the weasel leaves, Roger comes up and squirts dishwater out of his mouth. Bob Hoskins was pantomiming the handcuff all the time and George had a rig devised to pop up out of the sink and spout water. At the proper moment, the rig came up and shot water out and then dropped back down so Roger could do the rest of his action. Planning the shots out beforehand was the most important part so that everything could be covered by the animation - the rigs had to be made so they would fit exactly within the cartoon characters' dimensions. And since every shot had to be planned this carefully, if vas a real nut house \" From Bob Zemeckis' viewpoint, capturing all of this madness CINFFEY 35 • 131"],["14","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","14 - CINEFEX 35"],["15","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","was a major challenge. \"Things that we don't even think about on a normal movie we had to think about here - like the camera operator. I had to give direction to the camera operator and then he had to actually perform. Usually when an actor walks across the room, the camera follows - that's what's been done for eighty some years now. But here we had situations where a door would open and we would have to follow a nonexistent rabbit until he reached a chair that was rigged to move. Okay. How long does it take the rabbit to get from the door to the chair? 'Try it once, Ray. Let me see what it looks like.' I watch my monitor. 'Well, it needs to be a little faster.' Then the effects guys have to watch. Okay, the rabbit comes in. Action! One effects guy cues the device that opens the door. That in turn cues the camera operator. Then the guy who cues the chair has to take his cue off the camera. To keep all of this straight, we used every bit of technology that we have today, including elaborate video taps and monitors for the puppeteers. If we had been making this film back in the 1940s when there was no such thing as video, it would have been very difficult to cue all of these things.\" If all this was taxing for the director, it was equally as difficult for Bob Hoskins who had to work amidst the physical effects and imagine they were being caused by very real Toons. \"When I first took on this job, I watched the movies that had tried to do this in the past - Mary Poppins, Bedknobs and Broomsticks, Anchors Aweigh, Song of the South, Pete's DragonI saw them all. What I realized was that there was no actual relationship between the actors and the cartoon characters. The cartoon characters were there, but they didn't really exist because it was apparent that the actors couldn't really see them. For this movie to work, I had to see Roger. So I studied my daughter Rose - who was three at the time - and what I came to realize is that as you get older your imagination goes further and further to the back of your head. When you're very young. it's so close you can take it out and look at it. Rose has invisible friends that she plays with, so what I had to do was spend hours with her sort of developing this technique to actually see - hallucinate, virtually - to conjure these characters up. That's the first problem - to actually create the character, to make it live. Another consideration is the fact that an actor automatically fills up the space that's allotted to him. If there are two of you, you fill up half the space - but if you're acting alone, you take it all. So I began looking at ventriloquists. With a good ventriloquist, the act is shared - it's a double act. A bad ventriloquist either hides behind the dummy or comes out in front of the dummy. So like a good ventriloquist, I had to work on sharing my scenes with these animated characters.\" To help Hoskins with the process, stand-up comic Charles Fleischer provided the voice of Roger Rabbit live on the set. Each day, Fleischer would stand off-camera and feed Hoskins lines. \"Charlie Fleischer and I sort of developed this new acting technique that was quite extraordinary. Charlie himself is quite extraordinary. He turned up the first day and said: 'Where's my costume? Makeup?' We said, 'Charlie, you just do the voice.' And he said: 'So? I want a costume.' So they had to make him one. He would turn up in the morning with his costume on and makeup, and we would act together. Although he was on the other side of the camera, I was able to talk to him as if he were right next to me. We were close enough that we could ad-lib. I could even dance around with him - although he'd be dancing with me on the other side of the camera.\" The biggest challenge for Hoskins was maintaining a uniformly high level of concentration for the five months required to complete principal photography. Toward the end of the shoot, his approach started to get the better of him. \"I lost control of it at the end. I would hallucinate the Toons in very weird places - like in restaurants off the set. My daughter sort of realized this and said, 'Hey, Dad, I taught you how to do this, but don't go over the top!\" One of the film's most elaborate locales was the Ink and Paint Club - a notorious nightclub where Toons perform for human patrons. Even the help is animated - the doorman is a gorilla, the bartender is an octopus and the waiters are all penguins. \"We had that set built quite high with different levels underneath,\" Gibbs recalled. \"Then we had slots in the floor and special rigs for the trays. Guys would walk under the floor and hold the trays up on rods that had levers on them to make the trays move up and down and do all sorts of things. There were different trays for different things too - some were for food, some for drinks. We had video monitors rigged for the guys holding the rods so they could see where they were going.\" The penguins proved to be very professional waiters - complete with natural tux and tails - but what nightclub would be complete without a cigarette girl like Betty Boop? \"We had a smashing tray for Betty Boop. We made it a real miniature one and we were actually going to use the robotic arm we made for Baby Herman. We had a scene where Valiant asks for some cigarettes and she was going to hand him a pack and then give him a light. But then for some reason it was decided that Valiant should not be a smoker, so we didn't get to do that gag.\" The octopus bartender required many different approaches. Using wires, strings and nine puppeteers above the bar, Gibbs was able to make the octopus take money from a patron using one of his tentacles while lighting a woman's cigarette with another. At the same time, the octopus also wipes down the counter with a rag, takes a glass off a rack above, shakes a martini and serves a beer. All of the devices used to create these different effects were of course later concealed by animation. In screen time, the octopus shot goes by in a second or two, but it actually took a full day to film. Coproducer Frank Marshall - doubling as one of the film's second unit directors - was responsible for the shot. \"It was designed to be just a regular shot from any movie. If there had been a real bartender there, it would have been just an establishing shot of the bar. That's what we always tried to do - just create normal shots without calling particular attention to the fact that Toons were doing these things.\" A nonarticulated Baby Herman doll - used only for sizing and lighting - sits adjacent to a complex servocontrolled robotic arm constructed by Gibbs and his mechanical effects unit. Very compact for concealment beneath the infant animation, the robotic arm was capable of quite intricate action - including the handling of Baby Herman's ever-present cigar. / A larger robotic arm was constructed for Roger Rabbit and equipped with a remote control slave system that enabled a human operator to actuate the mechanical limb simply by moving his own arm into the desired position. / Though Hoskins worked only with empty air and a full imagination when cameras were rolling, both he and Zemeckis found it useful to rehearse with a full-size rubber dummy. Once a printed take was achieved, the poseable figure was walked through a nearidentical reference take to provide the animators with vital information as to character size and lighting. CINEFEX 35 • 15"],["16","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","With his weasel henchmen and a vat of 'dip' - a Toondissolving blend of benzene Lloyd) sets out to destroy Roger Rabbit as part of his Toontown off the map and freeways. For location work effects supervisor Michael Lantieri modified a vintage patrol van so that it could be cabin area vacant for later weasels. / Lantieri and his crew also modified two offroad vehicles as stand-ins for an animated taxi named Benny. Benny became the and turpentine - the evil Judge Doom (Christopher convoluted plan to wipe replace it with concrete in Los Angeles, physical driven remotely from the rear - thus leaving the insertion of the cartoon first assignment for an auxiliary animation unit formed in Los Angeles - under supervisor Dale L. Baer - when it became apparent that the London animators needed reinforcements to complete the massive workload on schedule. 16 • CINEFEX 35 The bartender, penguins and Betty Boop are mere window dressing compared to what Valiant sees when he first enters the club - Daffy Duck and Donald Duck on stage in a whirlwind jam session. Daffy is playing a white upright piano, while Donald is pounding away on a black baby grand. The two battle back and forth, each trying to upstage the other. Finally, Daffy hurls Donald into the upright and takes over control of the grand. As Daffy continues to play furiously, the lid of the upright slowly opens, revealing Donald inside with a cannon pointed directly at his adversary. \"This scene was one of those cases where you overshoot because there is no real way to know exactly what you need,\" Marshall explained. \"That was a problem on this movie - especially here, because I had nothing to go by. At least the first unit scenes had an actor in them - there was no one in the duck scene. We had this song that we knew was a particular length, and from that we had to sort of invent Toonstyle action that the ducks could be doing during their battle. east a long lue: We also had to rig the pianos to react to their antics. When Daffy knocks Donald into the white piano, its lid had to open, his chair had to pop up as he jumped off and other physical things had to happen on cue. We did this in the master and then we had to duplicate the same action from different angles. My biggest disappointment was the slam dunk. I had this great slam dunk devised where Daffy rolled Donald up into a ball, dribbled him over and slam dunked him into the upright. It was my homage to the Los Angeles Lakers, but it didn't make it in.\" Without human actors in the shots, the resulting footage - standing alone - could be more than a bit perplexing for the editors and animators. \"You'd look at our footage and see the camera move to one spot where something would happen, then move to another spot where something else would happen. We were afraid that down the line - six or eights months later - the animators or the optical guys would look at this footage and have absolutely no idea of what we had in mind while we were over there in London going crazy with this stuff. So we always shot a reference at the end of each shot to show what was supposed to be happening. Those were great fun to shoot because we all got to act out the duck parts.\" As the musical battle escalates, the piano keys - rigged to play the actual notes of Liszt's Hungarian Rhapsody - needed to quicken their pace correspondingly. To accomplish this, Gibbs and composer Alan Silvestri developed a computerized system that enabled the pianos to play at increasingly higher speeds - a far more complicated system than your average player piano. Crucial to achieving the effect was a synclavier - an electronic organ of sorts that can simulate any musical instrument. A specially designed circuit board converted the synclavier's digital impulses into analog electrical impulses. These impulses were in turn relayed to small solenoids underneath each key, Whenever a solenoid received an impulse, the piano key connected to it would depress accordingly. \"The theory was good,\" said Marshall, \"but since the number of cycles in England is different than the number of cycles here, we found that when we plugged our system in, the pianos did not play at the right speed. Also we discovered that you had to have one hundred percent pressure on the keys to get them to really look like they were playing. The piano keys had been keyed to the pressure of real fingers hitting the keys on the synclavier; so when part of the song called for a light touch, the key only went down a little bit. I wanted every key throughout the number to go down with a heavy touch because we were going to have big Toon fingers pushing them. As a result, Alan Silvestri had to spend a whole day furiously reprogramming the entire song while we shot angles on the scene that didn t involve the pianos.\" Once Daffy and Donald finish their recital, Roger Rabbit's luscious human Toon wife, Jessica Rabbit, appears on stage and delivers a show-stopping musical number in a smooth and sultry voice guaranteed to curl the hair of any self-respecting male - human or Toon. The scene also presents some remarkable interaction as Jessica slinks up to Marvin Acme, tweaks his cheeks, pulls a handkerchief out of his pocket, buffs his head with it and then lets it drop. Jessica next steps over to Eddie Valiant, sits in his lap, takes his hat off, pushes it playfully in his face and then gets up and walks away. To put it simply, Jessica is one helluva dame - with the seductive speaking voice of Kathleen Turner and the singing voice of Amy Irving. Moreover, she has an hourglass figure so rone one that n pronounced that a whole different approach had to be taken to achieve the live-action plates required for the sequence. \"Jessica's waist is so thin and her bosom is so large,\" Ralston noted, \"that there was no way we could roto over a real girl. Yet her movements were too precise to be done with wires or strings.\" Ultimately, Ralston came up with a solution - they would use half of a real girl. Serving as a stand-in for Jessica, diminutive actress Betsy Brantley was engaged to step through the carefully preplanned choreography - interacting as required with actors Bob Hoskins and Stubby Kaye. Meanwhile the action was being recorded with a field motion control system. Once the camera move was established, Ralston and his crew rephotographed the scene - but without the girl. Later an optical split-screen was employed to replace the lower portion of Brantley's body - leaving only her upper torso in the shot. Then the animators could cover her hands and arms with artwork and provide their version of Jessica's bust and waistline without concern for true body geometry. \"We also did some match moves on different ing chetts as lighting effects as she passes in and out from backlight to the spotlight and things like that. We needed separate elements for those so the light would flare in front of her face instead of just behind a matted-in image of her.\" While the Ink and Paint Club required the most complex set built for the film, the most difficult sequence to photograph was not at the studio but at the interior location site of the Acme gag factory - an old brick building situated in West London, which for many years had been an electrical power substation for the underground train system. \"The building was"],["17","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","hundreds of feet long, a hundred feet wide and maybe another hundred feet high,\" recalled effects cameraman Scott Farrar. \"It was also condemned, so part of the effort was put into rehabilitating the structure so that it wouldn't fall down on us while we were in there.\" Once reinforced and dressed as a set, the building would serve as the locale for a desperate battle between Eddie Valiant and the evil Judge Doom. In the scene, Doom unveils a specially designed pumping vehicle filled with enough 'dip' - a Toon-dissolving blend of turpentine and benzene - to erase Toontown and all its cheery inhabitants from the face of the earth. When Roger tries to intervene, he and Jessica are tied up and hung from a hook near the truck. Then, as Valiant struggles with the forces of Doom, the pumping mechanism is turned on and off and on again, threatening Roger and Jessica with death by dip. \"In the film it all goes by pretty quickly, but in reality it took seven weeks for the truck to move from one end of the building to the other. Because of the way the sequence was set up, we had to move it a little bit, shoot the master scenes and all the inserts, and then move on. Dean lit it to look like nighttime even during the day - and from inside, you never knew what time it was.\" \"The Acme factory was interesting because we operated with both a first and second unit,\" Watts added. \"We often do this on a film, but because the factory was a progressive set - one that deteriorated in a progressive fashion during the sequence - we were not able to do what we normally do, which is shoot the first unit and then let the second unit come in and do all the cleanup work. Instead we had the two units working around the clock, with the first unit working during the day and the second unit working at night. It's the first time I've ever operated a set more or less twenty-four hours a day. We made the decision to do so shortly after we started filming - and frankly, it was the only way to do it.\" One of the major props in the film was the giant dip truck - actually a heavily modified American-made World War II bridge building vehicle. \"When the war finished,\" said Gibbs, \"there were hundreds of these huge ten-wheel-drive trucks left in England and garage owners bought them to make breakdown vehicles. We found one ten miles from the studio and it was in very good condition. The engine was perfect, but since we were working inside the old power station we had to use something other than the original diesel engine because of the fumes. So we put in a really big twelve horsepower electric motor with a gearing mechanism and then drove the truck electrically. The dip truck was very complicated because there were hydraulics on it, electronics, flashing lights and a cannon going up and down and round and round with water squirting. It was so complex that we actually had an umbilical cord going to it with about sixty wires in it, all hooked up to a control console that operated everything.\" The dip sprayer mounted on top of the truck proved especially difficult because Gibbs wanted it to shoot out a tight laser-like beam. During preproduction he looked at a variety of CINEFEX 35 • 17"],["18","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","high-intensity water delivery systems - including some used to cut steel and concrete. Finally he found a unique nozzle employed in oil tankers to clean out the holds after crude oil has been emptied. \"This particular nozzle was scientifically designed with flow straighteners, so when we pumped water through at about a hundred and fifty pounds pressure the turbulence in it was rectified. It was just amazing because the water came out perfectly straight - a sixty-foot jet without any fall-off. This was terrific because it made the thing look really powerful - which it was - but the real beauty of it was that the camera and crew could get right up close to it without getting wet because it sprayed sixty feet in a perfect bar of water.\" Though the sprayer provided a coherent jet, the dip solution - a mixture of green fluorescent dye and water - invariably got all over everything. \"On second unit,\" Farrar noted, \"since we were shooting a lot of the stuff actually being sprayed out of the nozzle of the dip truck, the scenario would be that we would set up for a shot and actually spray the dip all over the walls and over the giant Acme toys and games. We would all stand around in 'wellies' - as they call rubber boots in England - and do the take. Then to do a retake, we had to wash everything down with water, get out large gas dryers to blow hot air around to dry everything off, sweep everything up, mush it all down the drains, clean up the floor, back everything up and start it all over again. It literally took several hours before we were ready for a second take. And this was the way we worked every night - it took forever!\" Another aspect of the sequence was a series of shots in which Judge Doom gets flattened by a steamroller. After accidentally punching a container of Acme glue while fighting with Valiant, Doom swings at his opponent and misses again, striking the front of the steamroller with his sticky fist which adheres instantly to the drum. Within moments, the steamroller rumbles over him, crushing him flat. To create this effect, Gibbs began by having actor Christopher Lloyd stand in a hole cut into the floor. \"The factory had a big space underneath the floor - about twenty feet deep - and the floor itself was made from about eight inches of concrete. We cut a rectangular hole in the floor and replaced it with plywood. Next we got Chris Lloyd and cut out the shape of him lying at about a ten-degree angle so that we had his shoulders and head above the board and his legs and part of his waist underneath the board. Then we had false legs made of tin and zinc which we married into his body. The whole thing worked really well because we made it appear as though he were lying flat on the floor. The legs were on springs and we had a couple of guys underneath the wood pushing them up and down so it looked like he was squirming and struggling. Then the roller ran over his legs and flattened them.\" However this is not the end of Doom. Valiant stares openmouthed as the flattened villain gets back on his feet - revealing in the process that he is actually a Toon in disguise. The paper-thin judge then walks across the factory floor and reinflates himself using an air bottle. To create the resurrection, 18 - CINEFEX 35 Jenáge JOLTON INDUSTRIES and Creator .F. God tech Coker Tire SUBWAY TERMINAL"],["19","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","three different steps were needed. Employed first was a flattened puppet that was radio-controlled with servos and actuators and featured a flat face with moving eyes and a pair of cracked glasses that fell off on cue. Then for a wide shot of Doom walking across the floor like a quivering saw blade, Tom St. Amand and Harry Walton at ILM shot an eighteen-inch stopmotion puppet. Finally, for the reinflation shot, Gibbs fashioned a flat latex replica of Lloyd and filled it with air by using a vacuum cleaner. When the inflatable puppet reached its intended size, its hat was rigged to pop off. With no further need to conceal his identity, the renegade Toon unleashes a full assault, transforming his hand into a bone-crushing anvil and then into a whirring buzz saw. Valiant manages to deflect his attacker and picks up a cartoon mallet with a hidden boxing glove inside. When the glove fires, it hits the main valve on the dip truck, blasting Doom with a full burst of spray that causes him to dissolve. In discussing the effect with Gibbs, Zemeckis had said he wanted a six-inch-diameter jet of dip to come out perfectly straight and powerful, knock Doom off his feet and propel him horizontally across the warehouse like a ping pong ball. To accomplish this, Gibbs rigged a stunt double on wires so that when the water shot out of the truck, it would hit the man and apparently knock him backwards about sixty feet. \"The problem was how to get Doom to dissolve in the dip,\" Gibbs noted. \"First we made a big puddle on the floor about four inches thick. In the center we had a cylinder built into the floor that was two feet in diameter and about eight feet deep. Inside it was a lift that we could slowly lower down to make Chris Lloyd look like he was dissolving in the dip - similar to the wicked witch in The Wizard of Oz. Then we had yellow smoke and other things underneath his cape.\" Principal photography wrapped in mid-April of 1987. For everyone involved, it had been a grueling five months. Yet despite the inherent difficulties, Zemeckis felt that the physical effects had gone smoothly. \"My experience is that when you make a normal movie - a movie that has all the actors in it, for instance - the things that usually are the most timeconsuming and frustrating are the special effects. On this film that wasn't the case. The effects crews had to run like the Marines because we had so many other problems that we couldn't wait while some guy rigged a wire on a door. There was never a glitch in the mechanical effects. Everything always worked. With Benny the Cab, we never had to wait for them to try to jump-start his engine because the battery was bad. When we had squibs that needed to be triggered, the squibs always fired. Everything went without a hitch. But then, I guess when you're working with the best effects man in Europe and the best effects man in the States, that shouldn't be too surprising.\" With live-action out of the way, Zemeckis and editor Arthur Schmidt returned to Los Angeles and began editing the movie - a task that proved immensely difficult. In fact, the two found themselves facing an odd sort of 'Catch 22'- the film had to be edited so that animation could be drawn to the selected takes, yet it was almost impossible to do that editing without the animation already there. \"The major problem was that we were waiting to put the costar into the film,\" explained associate producer and postproduction supervisor Steve Starkey. \"When you edit a special effects film like Star Wars, for instance, you have Princess Leia and Luke Skywalker in full body talking to each other and acting on a set. You may have to cut to their POVs or to shots of their ship in space, but never do you have to cut to an invisible costar for every scene. How do you build a movie around that? It would be like making Terms of Endearment without Jack Nicholson and all you have are shots of empty air into which you're going to add him later on. How do you cut the movie?\" Compounding the problem was the fact that the animation was not the only thing riding on a preliminary cut. Hoskins left the production after live-action was completed to do two other films, but he was committed to return the following December to finish up with three weeks of bluescreen work for the Toontown sequence. Exactly what Toontown would entail depended to a large extent upon what was ultimately edited to appear oking the spo before it. For Zemeckis, this period following the wrap of principal photography became the most agonizing of the entire production. \"You see, what I would do is say to myself, 'If the movie works without the animation then I know I'm really home.' But then the movie wouldn't work without the animation and I'd think I was failing. It was tough. Then I'd want to be a conscientious filmmaker, so I would sit in the editing room and say, 'Maybe I should cut this scene out.' And my editor would ask why. 'Because I don't think it works.' And he would say: 'But how do you know? There's no animation in it yet.' 'Oh yeah, you're right.' George Lucas told me something that helped when we were up at ILM in December. I had lunch with him, and I was really depressed, saying, 'You know, I can't figure out if this movie works or if it's any good.' And he said: 'Look, you've got to just realize - and I've done a lot of these - that you're never going to know until that final shot is in. Your movie will never really be finished until it's finished.' And he was right.\" Getting the film finished was a lengthy process involving editorial, animation and then optical printing, Once a scene was edited, it would be shipped to ILM where every frame would be photostated. These high-quality xerox enlargements would then be sent to the animators assigned to that particular scene. At the same time, Zemeckis, Ralston, Schmidt and Starkey would all fly to London to discuss the scene with its designated artists. \"This was the hardest work I've ever done,\" Zemeckis recalled. \"I would have to sit for hours and redirect the movie. The rabbit team would come in with Richard, and I would run the scene and then act out what the rabbit was doing. There would be a shot of an empty chair and I would say, 'Now the rabbit is sitting there and he is sad.' Then the animators would say, 'Well, how sad is he?' And I'd say, 'Well, not too sad, but kind of sad.' What they would do then is draw it real fast and say, 'Does he look like this?' And I'd go, 'No, he's a little sadder Clutching a fake steering wheel, Bob Hoskins sits perched atop the modified off-road vehicle while a stunt driver slung underneath maneuvers the oddball conveyance through downtown Los Angeles streets retrofitted to look like 1947. In postproduction, the Lantieri constructs - one of which could stretch out three feet when it accelerated - became the framework over which the animated taxicab was drawn. / Unable to make change, Eddie Valiant hitches a ride on the tail end of an electric streetcar - actually a contemporary bus outfitted by Dean Jeffries. Bogus street tracks and overhead cables cemented the impression that the dieselpowered vehicle was in fact a period trolley car. CINEFEX 35 • 19"],["20","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","Hiding out in a back room at the Terminal Bar, Roger does his best to resist Doom's uncovering fugitive Toons. Even subdued and nighttime lighting conditions were reproduced on the cartoon characters. To make them seem all the more a part of technique was developed in the ILM optical department tinting to shaded areas and shadows. / Valiant offers Roger a parting drink just moments before he is to be clasping empty air for most which Roger accepts the glass from Valiant required servo-mechanical robot arm sure-fire method for their live-action surroundings, a new to add appropriate color dunked in dip. / Though Christopher Lloyd was of the scene, the shot in the employment of the which would later be covered over by the rabbit animation. 20 * CINEFEX 35 than that.' Then they'd scratch that out and redraw it. It was really wonderful. At the same time, Richard drew everything and Steve Starkey took down extensive notes.\" Once the session was over, Zemeckis and crew would return to Los Angeles to edit the next scene. \"Every morning I would get a videotape of line drawings from London in a Federal Express pouch. I would look at the pose tests on video, call London and say, 'That's great,' or 'No, it's not right.' I would talk about the performance of the characters, never the animation - that was Richard's job. It was up to him to talk to the animators about quality. My concern was performance and making sure they were accommodating the live-action camera the way I had always pictured it.\" The painfully slow process was mired further at the outset because many of the animators had difficulty adapting to the live-action medium. Accustomed to creating an entire universe on a blank sheet of paper, the Roger Rabbitanimators suddenly found themselves forced to adhere to established camera angles and a more restrictive environment. As a result, Zemeckis and Williams had to teach them to see their work from a different perspective. \"The hardest thing for me,\" said Williams, \"was getting a new animator and turning his mind around to be able to work with the rotos in the right way. In some respects they had to forget about what they knew and learn to adapt to the film - to draw the baby over the robot arm or to turn the cartoon character with the camera. A lot of guys at first said it was impossible, but I would say: 'Try it. It really isn't, once you get into it.' In a way, the inexperienced animators did somewhat better because they didn't know all the old rules. They didn't know how to say, 'Oh, this isn't how it's supposed to be done. So there was a learning curve at first, but once everyone got programmed they just took off and went with it.\" If working on Roger Rabbit was a learning experience for the animators, it was also for members of the live-action team who learned to appreciate some of the freedoms animation had to offer. \"Animation does things the other way around from us,\" Robert Watts observed. \"For one thing, they start with the voice track before they do anything - that's very different. The one great thing we learned about cartoons is that they are elastic - so you can sometimes cover up a mistake by having a cartoon character stretch his arm ten feet out if you want him to, whereas you obviously cannot do that with a human being.\" Bob Zemeckis was similarly impressed. \"I loved Roger Rabbit's classic clown pyramid shape, but I kept worrying about his feet. I would say to Richard, 'What am I going to do if there's not enough space for his feet to get in between the coffee table and the desk?' Richard would say, Then we'll make his feet smaller in that shot and he'll walk right through.' You can do that with animation. That's what's so great about Toons - you're not locked into the physical reality because they can change their shapes and you never see it because it's something you just accept with animation.\" Who Framed Roger Rabbit ultimately required two animation teams. Williams personally oversaw a crew of more than three hundred in London, while Dale L. Baer directed an additional seventy-five in Los Angeles. The L.A. unit was brought on later in the production when it became clear that the London team would not have time to complete the enormous workload. \"The problem was that we couldn't find enough talented animators who would relocate to London to finish the show,\" Zemeckis stated. \"We would have preferred to have kept all the animation under the same roof - with the same camera and the same system - but there just were not enough talented people willing to shut down their houses and move to London for nine months. There was, however, a core group of very talented people who were more than willing to work on the movie if they could stay in Los Angeles - so that's why we put together the L.A. unit. It was a way to expand the animation unit and still get the best people in the world. Dale Baer and his crew were responsible for Toontown and for the Benny the Car sequence, and they did a terrific job.\" \"We had to sit down and start producing footage from the very first week on,\" Dale Baer recalled. \"Benny was our first assignment and he had his own set of problems. Hoskins was in this little tiny go-cart machine that would stretch for exaggeration and then condense - and no matter what it was doing, we had to cover every piece of it. Plus we were told not to follow the go-cart shape exactly. They wanted Benny animated. They e go-cart sead fuming wanted his head turning, looking up at Bob Hoskins and Roger in the car and anything else we could do. So we had to keep Benny alive all the way through, which was pretty tricky - especially since we had to design him in such a way that no matter what he did he was always covering up everything, including the driver in the back. This was the difficult part, because sometimes the driver would react to turning a corner or something and he would lean way out over the side of the car. We had to constantly lose him somehow.\" Unlike their compatriots in London, the L.A. animators had a decided advantage in that Zemeckis was nearby for most of the eight months they were on the picture. \"We had all our turnover meetings at Amblin,\" said Baer. \"We worked directly with Bob Zemeckis, Artie Schmidt and Steve Starkey - and with Ken Ralston who would come down and talk over animation effects with our effects supervisor Don Paul. It was easy on our end of it. It was more difficult when Bob would fly to London for a week or so. Then we would have to pouch video tests on a daily basis and wait several days to get a reply back. So we tried to bombard Bob with as much as we could during the times when he was in Los Angeles.\" Invaluable to both teams was the extensive preproduction research Richard Williams had done on how the various cartoon celebrities looked during the 1940s. With Bugs Bunny, for example, Williams assembled several large sheets covered with all the Bugs permutations in evidence in 1947 - the Chuck Jones ones, the Bob Clampett ones and the ones by Robert McKimson. Then when a scene called for Bugs to make an"],["21","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","CINEFEX 35 • 21"],["22","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","22 4 CINEFEX 35 THIS FOOD IS FOR THE THE CONVENIENCE OF STOMERS OUR DA NO CREDIT CASH ONL"],["23","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","appearance, these sheets were sent to the animator assigned the task who was then allowed to select the style he personally liked the best. Researching Mickey Mouse and Donald Duck and the other Disney stars was easier since all Williams had to do was go to the source. \"The morgue at Disney is the most marvelous place in the world for a serious animator. If you want to know how Bambi ran around in the thicket or how Thumper bit his foot, there it is. You can just go to that scene and pull out the original drawings, because all of them are there. And since this was a Disney movie, when we had to animate Dumbo and were working out the flap of his ears, we would get three scenes of him flying and either crib from them directly or make them better, which we attempted to do.\" Williams' goal in doing all this research was to make certain that the animation in Roger Rabbit was firmly rooted in the late 1940s. When the opening cartoon called for a kitchen setting, for instance, Williams researched kitchens of the period to insure that he and his staff would avoid any anachronisms. Interestingly enough, the original script and the novel on which it was based were both set in contemporary times. Zemeckis changed this, not only to add a sense of timelessness to the film, but also to avoid a potential clash of animation styles. \"I felt that we had to draw a line on the animation somewhere, and the perfect place to do that was at television. I didn't want to have to involve the TV characters like Fred Flintstone because they would not have looked right. All of the Toons in our film had to be fully animated, and television features limited animation. In a way, I wish we could have included the limited animation characters because my personal favorites are Rocky and Bullwinkle and the Jay Ward guys - but they wouldn't have fit in with the rest of the characters. I don't know what Rocky would have looked like with a bunch of tone mattes and shadows on him.\" One character Williams did not have to research was Roger. He did, however, have to provide reference sketches for other animators since there was no way he alone could have animated all the Roger Rabbit scenes. Because Roger appears throughout the film, a lot of different animators worked on him - and while audiences would be hard-pressed to notice, Williams was able to detect changes in the rabbit's appearance from scene to scene. \"Bob Zemeckis would say: 'Isn't it great? It's just this one rabbit. How did you do that?' And all the animators would say, 'No, Simon Wells' rabbit is different from Andreas Deja's rabbit, and both of those are different from Phil Nebbelink's.' One of our animators - Nik Ranieri - is a tall, thin Italian Canadian, and he had one scene in there where I said, 'Boy, that's a really good tall, thin Italian Canadian rabbit.' That one we ended up having to redo a bit, but most of the differences are undetectable to the average viewer. Those of us who worked on the characters can tell, but apparently nobody else can. They say that at Disney there were all these different Pinocchios - but we wouldn't know, would we?\" Back in the editing room, Zemeckis continued to review With only a small measure of success, Eddie Valiant attempts to conceal Roger beneath his trenchcoat as he enters the Terminal Bar in search of a hideout. To create the effect of the rabbit kicking and squirming inside the garment, Bob Hoskins was fitted with a special rig manned by off-camera operators. / Effects technicians prepare the actor for his scene. / Optical supervisor Edward Jones examines a test composite at ILM. More than a thousand opticals were required for the film - totaling some fifty-seven minutes of screen time. / Unconvinced of the need for subterfuge, the irrepressible Roger pops out of Valiant's coat to express his displeasure at being confined. / The plot thickens as Eddie finds himself succumbing to the considerable charms of Jessica Rabbit. CINEFEX 35 • 23"],["24","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","With rubber stand-ins for Roger and the weasels - and a live one for Jessica - Bob Zemeckis rehearses a factory with Bob Hoskins. Fleischer provided voices for Though he never appeared showed up daily in a rabbit costume to feed lines and Hoskins. / After five months of principal photography, hiatus while Zemeckis and editor Arthur Schmidt began assembling the footage so that the animation effort could begin in earnest. Eight months later, the company reconvened at ILM for three weeks of bluescreen work required for the penultimate sequence in which Eddie Valiant pursues his case into the heart of Toontown - a environment where even the scene in the Acme gag Stand-up comic Charles Roger Rabbit and several other Toon characters. on camera, the comic act through scenes with the main unit went on the arduous task of totally animated buildings are alive. 24 CINEFEX 35 pencil sketches while working with Schmidt to piece the rest of the film together. Once Zemeckis approved the initial pencil sketches for a given scene, an animation cameraman photographed them against the live-action photostats and sent the footage to editorial to be cut into the workprint. At the same time, a copy was also sent to Ken Ralston who would then review it with a technical eye. \"Ken would approve or reject on his level and I would approve or reject on my level,\" stated Zemeckis. \"Once it was approved all around, the shot went into final production - which was inking and painting. You're okay up to that point. You haven't spent a lot of money. The pencil sketches are fully animated, but if you pull the plug on them, you've still spent only about thirty percent of the cost of your animation minute. It's when you go into your ink and paint and your roto and tone mattes that it gets expensive.\" As the animators became more adept at putting their characters into the live-action environments, the footage count rose and more and more completed shots began filtering into editorial. Soon the Toontown bluescreen shoot loomed ahead, though there were still many aspects of the sequence that needed to be resolved. \"When we got into the latter phases of cutting,\" Starkey recalled, \"the one big question mark was this her that realed insert banner that read, 'Toontown sits here.' We knew by then that when the cartoons were in the real world, we were pulling it off. We had tested the ground and we knew we could make that work. But even in November, we hadn't yet finished the storyboards for this sequence called 'Toontown' where Valiant finally goes into a cartoon environment.\" \"When we started talking about Toontown,\" said Frank Marshall, \"everybody had a million ideas of what should happen, because we could do anything once we were inside cartoonland. But this was another danger - we didn't want the gags to overpower the advancement of the story. There were many things to deal with. How long is the Toontown section? What happens in Toontown? Nobody knew for a long time. And then, of course, all of us had seen bluescreen that just didn't look very good, so we were worried about that too. Actually, though, in addition to being a nightmare waiting for us, Toontown was a blessing in disguise because any spots in the story that we felt needed shoring up we could shore up in Toontown.\" Unlike the rest of Roger Rabbit, where the animation was largely dictated by the live-action, the Toontown sequence demanded just the opposite. Here the live-action was dependent almost totally upon the animation. As a result, the animation had to be worked out - at least on a preliminary level - before the bluescreen shoot could be undertaken. As finally conceived, the sequence follows a reluctant Eddie Valiant as he tails Jessica through the Toontown tunnel and into a cartoon landscape awash with cheerful colors and happy characters. Proceeding on, the detective finds himself in a bustling metropolis where everything is alive - even the buildings. \"All we were told at first was that Toontown was where the Toons lived,\" recalled coordinating animator Jane Baer. \"It was totally up to us to come up with the design and look of the place. We got ahold of a fabulous layout man named Dave Dunnet, and under Dale's direction he started to produce a really far-out countryside with quilted landscapes and a castle. We encouraged him to go further and further and basically he came up with our main design. When we got into the town itself - with the talking buildings - we decided to make the whole thing as surrealistic as possible and just go crazy with it.\" Though Dunnet eventually had to leave Roger Rabbit because of a prior commitment to Disney's The Little Mermaid, layout department head William H. Frake III was able to imitate the already established style - and with aid from the Disney archives, soon brought the fantasy landscape fully to life. 'We were given total access to the Disney morgue,\" said Dale Baer. \"It cost the studio money to use characters not owned by Disney - and there was a limited budget for that - so we relied heavily on Disney for pre-1947 animation to populate the coun- \"Animator Dave Pacheco made lots of trips to the morgue,\" Jane Baer added, \"researching material and determining what was available and what we could use. We wanted to get very funny, weird stuff and work it in. We came up with some pretty amazing things, but you have to look closely. We have naked babies on birds from Water Babies, some silly rabbits from The Tortoise and the Hare, and then we had the Three Little Pigs and the Big Bad Wolf and Mickey's nephews.\" The countryside also featured new characters, including singing trees - animated primarily by Bruce W. Smith - and three hummingbirds who bid Valiant hello. Though based on characters in Song of the South, the hummingbirds were actually new animation done by Barry Temple. Distracted by the hummingbird welcoming committee, Valiant runs into Jessica's parked car. In order to provide a later rationale for Valiant and Jessica to hitch a ride with Benny, it was necessary to disable both of their vehicles - a plot device which presented a problem since Roger is unconscious in Jessica's trunk when she enters Toontown. It was therefore up to the animators to figure out a way of damaging the front of her car without crushing the back. \"The idea was to have Valiant hit her car and push it into something that disabled it,\" Dale Baer noted. \"We were thinking of having elephants fall on her car - or bowling balls or anchors or something. But by then they had already used almost every cartoon gag in the book - safes, pianos, anvils - so we thought, 'Instead of just one gag, why don't we use them all?' So her car gets pushed into the Toontown Overused Gag Disposal Truck and nearly everything imaginable falls on the front of it - an airplane, an I-beam, a piano and, naturally, the kitchen sink.\" Gags are the lifeblood of Toontown, and many were developed that eventually fell by the wayside because of money or time or demands of the plot. Early on, for instance, one scene called for it to rain cats and dogs. Another called for Valiant to look up the street and see the Toontown firehouse in flames with"],["25","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","crazy clown firemen running around trying to extinguish the blaze while struggling to catch an elephant jumping out one of the windows. \"Another gag that we loved involved Tex Avery's wolf,\" Jane Baer recalled. \"We had him coming into Toontown in one of those super-long cartoon cars that bends when it goes around corners. The wolf drives up to the curb in a big cloud of dust, screeches to a halt and drops off a passenger. Then he says, 'See ya, Toots,' and speeds away - and left standing there is Snow White brushing down her dress. That had to go because Disney did not want to tarnish her image.\" Snow White may not have made it into the Toontown sequence, but Disney's biggest star did - in a scene that also featured Warner Brothers' biggest star. Sighting a silhouette he takes to be Jessica Rabbit on the top floor of a Toontown hotel, Valiant endures a hair-raising elevator ride and then enters the room in question only to find the love-starved Lena Hyena. Fleeing her advances, the detective darts into a restroom. Suddenly he realizes that the room has no floor and he is actually standing in midair. In typical cartoon fashion, he remains in stationary suspension until the full gravity of his situation sinks in - then he starts to fall. Valiant thinks he is saved when he manages to grab hold of a flagpole, but Tweety - already on the pole - playfully peels his fingers away and sends him plummeting toward the concrete below. Suddenly Mickey Mouse and Bugs Bunny skydive into the frame. Valiant frantically asks if either of them has an extra parachute and Bugs obligingly offers him a spare - a spare tire, that is. \"In doing Mickey,\" Dale Baer recalled, \"I was most concerned that he look like Fred Moore's 1947 Mickey, Mickey is not an easy character to draw. The slightest change in some of his shapes changes his whole look. All of these character had a key person who drew them, and you always want to emulate the master. Mickey could only have come out of Freddie Moore at that time, and I wanted to honor him because he was the Mickey Mouse king. One of the most difficult things about drawing Mickey was the fact that Dave Spafford was drawing Bugs - and Dave doesn't like anybody to see his work until he's done with it. You can't second-guess what Dave is going to do. He's a genius and he has everything all locked up inside his head and he won't tell you what he's going to do. It all just comes out while he's doing it. But if Bugs and Mickey are in the same shot and you don't know where Bugs is, how do you know where to make Mickey look? I'd say to him: Tell me where Bugs is in frame 147. That's all I want for now. Just tell me where he is and I'll leave you alone.' So finally he'd give me that and I'd know where to make Mickey look.\" Other animators also worked on the sequence. Frans Vischer did all the animation on the hotel lobby, as well as the Dum Dum bullets Valiant fires down the street at one point. Droopy Dog, the elevator operator, was drawn by Mark Kausler - who also did Tweety. And Lena Hyena was created by Matthew O'Callaghan. Though none of the Toontown animation was accomplished until after the bluescreen shoot, the entire sequence was thorCINEFEX 35 • 25 VLEAR KEEP"],["26","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","26 - CINEFEX 35"],["27","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","oughly storyboarded by the time Zemeckis and crew reassembled on the ILM bluescreen stage in December. \"Bill Frake and I did extensive storyboards specifically for camera angles and for the shots themselves,\" noted Baer. \"I think we spent two weeks working on them - from like seven in the morning to seven at night. Then Bob Zemeckis would come in and go over things with us. Finally we got everything ready, and Bill went up to ILM with the storyboards to work with them during A video compositing system was employed at ILM to assist in the bluescreen photography. First the animation storyboards - or variations of them - were placed in front of a video camera and fed onto monitors manned by the director and key technicians. Then, with a video tap from the main Vistavision camera, Hoskins was videotaped against bluescreen and matted over the storyboards, thus providing the filmmakers with an approximation of how the final shot would look. \"The bluescreen shoot was really the reverse of what the rest of the film had involved,\" Zemeckis noted, \"because here we had a human in a bluescreen void. In the live-action scenes we used a rubber dummy of Roger to show the animators the size relationship. With the video system, we drew rough background sketches right at ILM to make sure we were placing the human in the frame at the correct size. We also had all the storyboards - which we embellished at the time or even changed, because like all storyboards these were sometimes drawn in ways that a camera could never really photograph.\" \"The system made it easier for us and it made their shooting go faster,\" Baer added, \"because when you're working with bluescreen, you have no idea where the horizon line is or where the floor plane is. The storyboards gave them a whole picture, so they could have Bob Hoskins' double walk through the shot and they could adjust the perspective on the layouts. Anything Bill had to adjust in the design of the layouts, he also did at this time.\" Besides getting a sense of Hoskins' place in the frame, the video system also helped Dean Cundey work with Frake to establish light sources in the shots. Cundey lit Hoskins on the bluescreen stage with a variety of different colored lights while Frake made corresponding sketches to insure that sources for those lights would appear in the background animation. \"Our Toontown bluescreen shoot was a nightmare,\" Ralston stated. \"We had so much to do and very little time to do it. In the movie, five minutes are spent in Toontown. That's a lot - especially for a series of bluescreen shots. We had to do something like a hundred and thirty shots, one right after another - which is more than we have done for entire movies before - and all for this five-minute sequence. In the end, though, we planned it so carefully that we actually got done a little bit ahead of time and saved some money.\" In Toontown, Eddie Valiant's body is subject to the non-Newtonian physics of cartoon animation, so it is not surprising that Bob Hoskins spent much of the bluescreen shoot dangling from one form of wire rig or another. \"When Bob was supposed to be hanging from For the filmmakers, Toontown presented a situation exactly opposite from the rest of the film. Instead of having cartoon characters interacting with live-action sets and props, there was now the need to have a live-action character interacting with animated sets and props. Expecting to find Jessica within, Eddie Valiant enters an upper-story Toontown hotel room. / Now subject to the screwball physics of animated cartoons, Valiant finds himself pinned to an elevator ceiling when operator Droopy Dog puts the conveyance into a rapid descent. / When the elevator comes to an abrupt halt, Valiant is flattened on the floor like a pancake. / No real sets were employed in the Toontown sequence. Bob Hoskins worked alone in a bluescreen vacuum - frequently on wire rigs - and was later composited into strictly cartoon settings. / Attempting to escape the advances of Lena Hyena, Valiant enters a room with no floor and - after a cartoonlike moment of midair suspension - plummets groundward past an animated building. CINEFEX 35 • 27"],["28","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","Animator David Spafford at work on a scene involving Daffy Duck. More than three dozen seasoned animators artists, in-betweeners, inkers technicians and cameramen. / Background artist Ron Dias prepares a color key for one of the Toontown settings. / William H. Frake III traveled to ILM during the bluescreen shoot and worked closely being shot. A video matting system enabled live imagery from the bluescreen stage to be composited in real-time over these sketches, thus affording Zemeckis and his key staff a vital reference for choreographing his action. Cundey to insure uniform lighting between the liveaction bluescreen elements and the animated settings. worked on the film - backed up by literally hundreds of animation assistants, background and painters, rotoscope Layout department head with the live-action unit, providing storyboard sketches and impromptu drawings as scenes were positioning Bob Hoskins within the frame and Frake also worked with cinematographer Dean 28 4 CINEFEX 35 the flagpole, he was actually suspended sideways about three feet off the floor. For a lot of the falling stuff we used a very long dolly - about eighty feet long - that went out through our big stage into a truck that we had set up. There were fans blowing on him and Bob would pretend he was falling as we raced down the dolly track with an eight-perf camera. It was hard, both for the guys on the dolly and for Bob. To be hanging in a flying rig for as long as he did can be painful - like having your shorts on too tight - but Bob was great. Besides giving a really incredible performance, he always put in a million percent. His entire being was into the part. He did anything anybody wanted - and always with a sense of humor.\" Even on the bluescreen stage Zemeckis was determined to break all the rules and not play it safe. \"What makes this movie work also makes it work in Toontown,\" Frank Marshall noted. \"Bob shot the bluescreen like a regular film. He did shots where he would start on Jessica, pull back and find Hoskins in the foreground. That's a shot you'd do in a live-action movie, but you'd never do that on bluescreen. And then Dean lit it in such a way that it wasn't flat like a lot of bluescreen. He matched lighting so that all the lighting that would be in the background animation would also be on Hoskins.\" When the bluescreen shoot was finished, photostats of the shots were sent to the L.A. unit where supervising background artist Ron Dias had to incorporate the live-action lighting scheme into his cartoon environs. \"Matching that lighting was quite a feat,\" Jane Baer commented. \"Ron's sense of color, in our opinion, is about the best in the business today. He would approved, he would do the big main keys with the help of Michael Humphries and Kathleen Swain. Cindy Finn also worked closely with Ron to get all the cartoon characters to work within his backgrounds.\" Aside from the live-action bluescreen session, ILM effects stages were largely uninvolved with Roger Rabbit. A few miniature shots were the only notable exceptions. Employed for a series of point-of-view shots and background plates was an interior representation of the lengthy tunnel extending from the real world of Los Angeles into the cartoon world of Toontown. \"The tunnel was a model laid out by Jeff Olson,\" stated supervising modelmaker Steve Gawley. \"It was built out of wood and detailed with plaster to look like bricks on the inside. The brick texturing was painted and aged, and we put in little pipes, ceiling lights and other details to make the tunnel look more real. The model was about twenty feet long, with removable ceiling sections - about a foot long each - that could be removed sequentially as we dollied the camera through at a speed that appeared to be about twenty-five miles per hour.\" Also constructed was a scaled-down replica of the wall inside the Acme factory which is rammed by the out-of-control dip truck at the end of the film. \"Originally this was supposed to be done on the live-action set in England,\" Gawley continued, \"but they wanted the truck to drive all the way through the wall and disappear, and the full-size set was not big enough for that. They did drive the real truck partially through the set and got good closeups of the action, but our wall was used for long shots. Ira Keeler and I built it out of plaster - actually individual bricks made out of plaster so they would break apart in scale. Part of the wall was made solid, with a gag hole placed in the center. All the bricks were glued on individually. We used sixteen thousand plaster bricks in all, and the actual breakaway part involved about six thousand bricks. The model dip truck was built in England and was about five feet long and two feet high. Everything on it moved, from the dip gun to the rollers and the sweepers.\" The wall was constructed in a horizontal position and the gag hole was filled in - three bricks thick and tightly packed. \"We covered the completed wall with a piece of plywood and carefully stood it up vertically. Plywood on either side of the wall held the bricks in place. Then we filled the background of the set with smoke and rigged debris to fall from above after the dipmobile was pulled through on a wire. We did one take and it worked great, but we had to shoot it again because we didn't know to wet the wall with dip and we needed more dip on the floor. It was very time-consuming to restack all those bricks. Fortunately, the second take worked out.\" In addition to the Toontown tunnel and the brick wall, the model shop also provided an eighteen-inch miniature of the Toon Patrol car for some bluescreen shots, a period phone stand, a Toon .38 caliber handgun and two Vistavision camera blimps. Once the bluescreen shoot was completed and the animation was fully underway, attention turned more and more to the ILM optical department where the live-action and animation would be combined. \"This year has been a milestone for us,\" noted optical supervisor Ed Jones, \"not only for Roger but because we had to get Willow through at the same time. We had to do approximately thirteen hundred composites in eight months. To put that in context, during the past ten years at ILM we've done a total of thirty-five hundred optical composites - so if you divide thirteen hundred into thirty-five hundred, you can see that we have basically had to do almost three-and-a-half years' worth of work in eight months. Needless to say, we were running twenty-four hours around the clock to accomplish this - and we had a staff of over thirty.\" While the workload alone was staggering, Jones' biggest challenge came from developing a new technique for combining animation with live-action. \"We started out doing our early test to sell the idea to Disney executives that we could use interactive light to marry the animation and live-action. For the first test Kenneth Smith just put regular two-dimensional flat animation into the scene, placing it behind windows and trashcans and things. Also, if there was a real source of light in the scene, we would bounce that light source off the cartoon character's head. When this proved successful with Roger, we then did another test that involved Jessica where we decided to take it a little further. We wanted to start to sculpture Jessica using"],["29","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","CINEFEX 35 • 29 ROSE DOn"],["30","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","30 - CINEFEX 35 POURNTREUE. POLICE"],["31","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","what we call clear centers - which are just clear against black. In the past, we have used gray tones to add shadows to an animated character or object. Initially Bruce Vecchitto and I did the same thing with Jessica, using different tones of gray to create shadows and highlights. This test was very successful, but we wanted to try something that hadn't been done before.\" Ralston and Jones decided to use colored shadows on the animated characters to give the Toons color highlights like those on the human actors in the scene. Jones and Peg Hunter started the process with another test on Jessica, establishing a pastel palette of colors on color print stock that would blend well with her Toon tones. \"Say Jessica is standing half in a shadow in one scene,\" Jones explained. \"That means her face should be lighter on one side and darker on the other. If we followed tradition, we would have darkened half her face using a shade of gray. In this case, we used a darker flesh color. Since her normally orangish hair also had to be partially in shadow, I chose a darker shade of orange to alter that and, since her dress was red, I went more toward a burgundy or a deeper red. And if she were standing in front of a blue curtain, the shadow on the blue curtain would also be a darker shade of blue. To my knowledge, no one has ever done that before - we've certainly never done anything like it at ILM - but we felt that we had to do something different for Roger Rabbit because we didn't want it to look like the animation in Mary Poppins or Pete's Dragon. What we came up with we call '23⁄4-dimension' - basically sculpting two dimensional animation with color to create a three-dimensional look and thus marrying the animation into the scene.\" Making the Toon characters look '23⁄4-dimensional' - but not quite three-dimensional - was important because the filmmakers agreed that the characters should not look too real. \"Our purpose,\" Ralston commented, \"was not to make something so ultra three-dimensional that it looked like a computer graphic or something these cartoon characters never were. We didn't want people to say, 'Hey, that's not the cartoon character I remember.' So we had to get an interesting cross between the animation we remember and now having them walk in and out of shadows and light. We had them in situations where light played on them in new ways, but they always had to remain essentially cartoons.\" When this approach proved successful with Jessica, Jones then had to do the same for the other Toons. \"Because of our deadlines, I never had much time for research and development. So when Roger started coming in here, I had to look at my palette of colors and say, 'Okay, which colors are we going to use for him?' Once again, we took a whole selection of colors and started wedging them over the top of Roger to see which ones worked best for him. It took about three weeks before we came up with a selection of colors that worked well through all the different sequences. Then Donald and Daffy came in and we had to select different colored shadows for them. And then the weasels came in and each one of them was different - Greasy was in a green suit so we had to come up with green tones for him, Smart Ass was pink so we had to use shades of pink for him, and then we had to develop brown tones for Stupid. The more animated characters that came in, the greater the number of colors we had to use. It became kind of fun, but it also created more problems for us because we had to sculpt the characters using color on top of color. Through experimenting, we learned what colors worked best in the different sequences with the different characters. We started out using about thirty-five colors, but eventually we narrowed it down and ended up using ten to fifteen colors throughout the film.\" Had Jones and his crew simply used shades of gray rather than color, they could have processed their tests in house. But with color, they were forced to use an outside lab, adding more time to the process. The switch to color also required more elements - up to fifteen on some of the shots with Jessica. One reason Jessica was so complex is because she is the only human-looking Toon in the film. As a result, her look had to be carefully created. \"After we did the colored shadows on Jessica, I was in Las Vegas and went to one of the shows there. I noticed that the entertainer was wearing a sequined dress and when the lights hit her in a certain way it was like flashbulbs going off. I thought we should give Jessica's dress that same quality in the nightclub sequence, so we had the animators do flash sequins. Then Ralph Gordon and Bruce Vecchitto in the optical department did a lot of different tests using steel wool over a plastic bag and came up with this nice star effect. We incorporated that into our wedges and came up with neat neon designs. We also wanted to give her dress a see-through quality, so we created mattes that did partial exposures on her dress. When all these different elements were combined, the dress looked like something you'd see on a real stage.\" The backdrop for Jessica's performance also provided numerous challenges. \"In the Ink and Paint Club there were a lot of different problems to consider. First there was a certain level of smoke. Then there were a lot of cartoon characters throughout the scene. The penguins are behind everything, carrying real trays, so Brad Kuehn had to have carefully articulated mattes around the edges of the trays and articulated mattes for when the penguins walked behind real people before he could complete the shots. Then with the octopus we needed all these articulates because he was handling a real cocktail shaker and a real cigarette lighter and all these things were held by mechanical arms that had to be articulated out. Each shot in that sequence provided its own challenges.\" \"Normally we just had one rotoscope element in any particular shot,\" added rotoscope supervisor Jack Mongovan, \"but with the bartender, I think we did seven different elements. As the camera pans down the bar, I created one articulate for the people and one for the objects on the bar that never changed position. Then there were all the objects the bartender was holding, like the cigarette lighter. The shot was lit so you couldn't see the wire that was holding it, but the octopus was Secured through the persuasive powers of executive producer Steven Spielberg were the proprietary rights to employ celebrated cartoon characters from the preeminent Walt Disney and Warner Brothers studios as well as several other purveyors of classic animation. Thus, for the first time ever, Disney cartoon stars such as Mickey Mouse and Donald Duck appeared side by side with classic Warner Brothers characters such as Bugs Bunny and Daffy Duck. / One of many Toontown settings rendered by Ron Dias. / A final showdown transpires in the Acme gag factory where Judge Doom's weasels temporarily get the drop on Eddie Valiant. CINEFEX 35 • 31"],["32","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","Judge Doom unveils the mechanism by which he intends to destroy Toontown - a giant spray truck filled with vast quantities of dip. The vehicle - fabricated by George Gibbs and his crew - was actually a vintage bridge builder. / For a later scene in which Doom is steamroller, the Gibbs unit constructed a mechanized puppet. / In preparation for the scene, Christopher Lloyd was lowered into a hole in the floor with only his arms and head left protruding. The mechanical lower body - complete with rod puppet legs operated from below - was then attached at the chest and run over by the vehicle. / Doom's encounter with the steamroller reveals masquerading as a human. Undeterred by the flattening, he rises and walks over to an air bottle to reinflate himself - an effect achieved in part with a go-motion puppet shot bluescreen. / Modelmakers Ira Keeler and Steve Gawley top off the tank on a scale model dip truck which is about to be driven through a miniature crushed flat by a that he is actually a reproduction of the renegade Toon factory wall. 32 4 CINEFEX 35 flicking the lighter and at times we had to take away the part of the octopus that was behind the lighter and make sure part of him stayed in front of it so it looked like he was actually holding it. He was also holding a shaker bottle, and I had to leave his fingers holding it but take out the part that was behind it. This kind of work is very tough. Sometimes I would go through a shot and get far along and then realize that I was looking at it wrong and had to go back and redo it. Then at different times the octopus went behind different glasses, so we had to do a distortion matte to make him look distorted behind the glass. Even though the entire shot was only two hundred frames long, it took almost a month to complete.\" The animation and rotoscope departments provided several additional effects including the portable Toon hole that was seen in the Acme factory. In the film, a policeman rummaging around the factory finds one of the ubiquitous holes and throws it up against a wall. In cartoon tradition, he then sticks his arm inside the hole. In reality, a breakaway wall section was used on the set - hinged to allow the actor to pass his arm through the simulated bricks. In postproduction, this then was covered by the animated hole - designed by animation supervisor Wes Takahashi and rendered by Sean Turner. Two years in the making, Who Framed Roger Rabbit is now in release and breaking boxoffice records. But its success was not a foregone conclusion - even for the filmmakers whose vision it embodies. \"The main thing about Bob Zemeckis,\" observed Richard Williams, \"apart from his smarts, is his tremendous courage. I can't imagine anybody I know who would take such a risk and shoot an entire movie with a hole in the middle of it - leaving that hole for the main character. Can you imagine that? When he would run this movie without the animation in it, it looked like a dog. He would get into terrible depressions. But as soon as the animation was put in, you could see that he had conceived each shot perfectly and that it was all working. But just imagine doing Hamlet without Hamlet. Every time Hamlet is going to come in, you hear a voice from off-stage - 'Alas, poor Yorick' - and nobody's there. Or maybe there's just a skull hanging on a string and Ken Ralston is going to paint the string out later. He had nothing there. That takes incredible courage, don't you think?\" \"Robert Watts had the greatest description of Roger Rabbit,\" Zemeckis concluded. \"He said, 'It's an epic movie - not because we're going to have an army of fifteen thousand people and blow up an entire village in one shot, but because what we're trying to do is have an animated rabbit pick up a crumb.' And he was right. A rabbit picking up a crumb is epic in its scope, and that is where the effects guys realized: 'Hey, wait. Anybody can blow up a building. All you need is a lot of power. But how do I do this?' That's why everybody was so jazzed by the challenge. It was tough going a lot of the time, but I always knew we had the one-two punch - we had the greatest animators in the world and we had the greatest effects people in the world. We were unstoppable.\""],["33","Who Framed Roger Rabbit","CINEFEX 35 • 33"],["34","Willow",""],["35","Willow","WILLOW article by Jody Duncan Shannon"],["36","Willow","decade after George Lucas conceived of it, Willow is an epic fantasy recounting the heroic adventures of Willow farmer unwittingly thrust into the midst of a powerful struggle between good and evil when a baby princess is placed in his care. The $35 project in several years - demand for abundant effects both large and small. / Hired to direct the film was Ron Howard who worked closely crew to remote locations in England, New Zealand and Wales. / Sworn to deliver the baby Elora Danan to safety in the kingdom of Tir Asleen, the gentle Willow (Warwick odyssey fraught with many dangers. Along the way he enlists the begrudging aid of and together they challenge who has vowed to murder Produced more than a Ufgood, a kindly Nelwyn million production - Lucasfilm's first major would stretch the capabilities of Industrial Light and Magic in its with executive producer Lucas throughout. Committing the story to film would take cast and Davis) sets out on an a Daikini warrior named Madmartigan (Val Kilmer) the evil Queen Baumorda 36 * CINEFEX 35 It was fifteen years ago, while researching the science fiction and fantasy genre in preparation for Star Wars, that the idea for an epic fantasy first came to George Lucas. Sidetracked in the years that followed - first by the Star Wars trilogy and later by his ongoing Indiana Jones series - Lucas finally brought his long-nurtured idea to fruition with the completion and release of Willow early this summer. Evoking classic themes of mythology and religion, the screenplay - by Bob Dolman from an original Lucas story - recounts the heroic odyssey of a tiny Nelwyn farmer named Willow Ufgood whose chronicle begins with the discovery of a baby girl afloat in the river near his home. Though Willow and his family take the foundling into their midst, the local townsfolk demand that the child be returned to her own people - a mandate that sends Willow and a small band of compatriots off on a long and perilous journey. En route, the party encounters a swarm of brownies - tiny forest dwellers less than a foot tall - who attack and imprison them until the fairy queen Cherlindrea appears and orders them set free. Cherlindrea informs Willow that the baby, Elora Danan, is actually a princess destined to bring about the downfall of the wicked Queen Bavmorda and entreats him to escort the child to safety in the kingdom of Tir Asleen. Equipped with her magic wand, and with brownies Rool and Franjean as guides, Willow sets out on his mission. In alliance with the sorceress Fin Raziel - bewitched and turned into a talking possum - and with intermittent help from the warrior one ramar gan the st rogue Madmartigan, the small but resolute Nelwyn braves the fearsome army of General Kael, encounters a two-headed firebreathing dragon and defies the potent black magic of Bavmorda to deliver the baby Elora to her noble destiny and defeat Though Lucas saw Willow as a story of friendship and courage rather than an effects extravaganza, the elements of fantasy and magic inherent in the tale were sufficient to more than challenge the capabilities of his celebrated Industrial Light and Magic facility. In fact, because of the variety and volume of the workload, the effects sequences in Willow were divided among a trio of cosupervisors - Dennis Muren, Michael McAlister and Phil Tippett - all of them Oscar winners. Though Muren served as overall coordinator, his primary areas of concern were the fairy sequence, the transformation of Raziel and the climactic battle of the sorceresses. McAlister meanwhile devoted most of his time to the multitude of brownie shots while Tippett tackled the two-headed dragon. Among the first to become involved with the project and its various characters was effects art director Dave Carson. Working closely with director Ron Howard - who had been initiated into the visual effects realm on Cocoon - Carson and his team of artists began design work on Willow while the script was still in treatment stage. \"Ron Howard came onto the project with no preconceived ideas. He was as curious to find out what all of these things were going to look like as anyone - and he had a sense of wonder and curiosity that was there from the beginning, It was nice working with him. As the conceptual phase progressed, there was more and more of an emphasis on trying to get things done in-camera as opposed to optically. George especially seemed interested Filmed on rugged and exotic locations in England, Wales and New Zealand, Willow would nonetheless rely heavily on the ILM matte department to enhance the majestic scenery, dark forests and medieval castles called for in the script. Unlike previous shows where the matte shots were carefully worked out in advance, a much more free-wheeling approach was adopted. \"There were over one hundred plates shot for possible matte paintings,\" noted matte department supervisor Christopher , \"but we ended up using only a few. It was a practical Evans, system for ILM, even though we were not involved in the plate photography. Starting with Vistavision plates as raw material, we worked with George to design the necessary matte shots. After going through all this raw material, we came up with some thirty-five shots - about five of which were dropped along the way. Once the plates were selected, we did a series of ten-byfour-inch acrylic paintings to establish the lighting and color and look of all the matte shots in the film.\" Establishing a geography for the Nelwyn homeland was an early concern. \"George liked the look of a region in southern China called Kweilin where there are many unusual limestone formations. The first series of matte shots was derived from this fantastic landscape. This was followed by a montage of the territory Willow and his friends cover on their trek to deliver the baby to the Daikinis. The idea was to suggest something strange and wonderful, yet dangerous at the same time. In editing the sequence, George realized that he needed locations that had not been found in New Zealand. Because Joe Johnston was no longer available, I filled in as second unit director for a couple of weeks, With Craig Barron as director of photography, we went off on location with the actors and shot our own plates for the new shots. Our first location was at Burney Falls, near Mount Shasta. Then we went to several locations around Marin County and at Skywalker Ranch. For the sunset scene, we shot the actors walking in silhouette along the edge of a hill. Then a cutout was made of some mountains and set up against the sky, and Craig Barron and Randy Johnson went out day after day to get a good sunrise. They finally got it - latent image in Vistavision - with a long lens at fifteen frames per second so the clouds would move by very quickly, Paul Swensen painted in snow-capped mountains and the resultant latent image composite was combined with the silhouettes in optical.\" To introduce an element of danger in the journey, one of the shots Willow photographs copyright © 1988 by Lucasfilm Ltd. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Keith Hamshere. Effects unit still photography by Kerry Nordquist and Sean Casey, Special thanks to Karen Dube, Gini David, Amy Moore and Elliott Chang."],["37","Willow","CINEFEX 35 • 37 PANAVISION ANAFLEX"],["38","Willow","38 - CINEFEX 35"],["39","Willow","called for the Nelwyns to cross a log bridge spanning a great chasm. \"We found on location that the little people had difficulty crossing logs and things like that, so for this shot we just photographed them against sky walking on the roof of a rental truck. Then we bipacked them as a silhouette image and dropped that in front of the painting. The only thing live in the whole shot is that silhouette - everything else was created.\" In addition to providing spectacular landscapes, the matte department helped to create both the Nockmaar castle of Queen Bavmorda and the castle at Tir Asleen. Early in the film, Bavmorda's castle is introduced in a long establishing shot. Though the surrounding volcanic landscape was shot at Tongariro Volcanic National Park in New Zealand, the castle itself was a painting by Caroleen Green. A closer establishing shot employed a miniature castle in combination with a matte painting. \"Mike Pangrazio and Craig Barron discovered a couple of years ago - while working on Young Sherlock Holmes and Starman - that there are real advantages to using miniatures with matte paintings. Miniatures have incredible perspective, but a matte painting can create a sense of atmosphere and distance better than a model. Combining the two techniques is like making an alloy in metallurgy - the combination of the two ingredients is stronger than either by itself. What we're talking about is not simply having a miniature and then putting a painting behind it. The miniature is shot latent image and then we add the paint to it - so it is actually a blending and intermixing of the two. As far as I can determine it has never really been done before - certainly not to this degree.\" Modelmaker Paul Huston - on assignment to the matte department - contributed the miniature castle, extrapolating from a giant partial set constructed at the location site by production designer Allan Cameron. \"The miniature castle was about six feet wide by four feet deep,\" said Huston. \"On the fullsize set there was a pattern of buttresses with wall sections in between that repeated around the entire castle. So I made two of those sections that were then molded by Wesley Seeds and cast up into about twenty-five pieces that I was able to assemble around the bottom. I also made some towers out of foam core, put styrene stone texture on them and then modified the texture a bit so that it looked more castle-like. The entire scale of the castle was based on finding that particular stone texture in styrene - otherwise most of our labor would have been in just creating that texture.\" In addition to building the castle - which took only five days to complete - Huston fashioned a forced perspective foreground piece complete with tiny skeletons and other evidence of battles lost and won. \"Eric Christiansen helped me with some of the details on the castle - the skeletons and skins hanging on poles on the outside of it. The castle has a lot of those things hanging around.\" A matte painting and miniature was also employed for a down-angle shot of a winding staircase within the castle. \"About a third of the frame was the circular staircase going down to infinity,\" explained Huston. \"I built a forced perspective minThe journey begins with a montage of matte shots as Willow and his small band depart the Nelwyn valley to return the precious child to her homeland. A magnificent sunset was shot latent image by matte cameraman Craig Barron, then enhanced with a painting of snowcapped mountains and a bipack element of the characters in silhouette. / Under the direction of matte department supervisor Christopher Evans, a crew was dispatched to several norther California locales to photograph additional plates for the sequence. Footage of the Nelwyn actors at Burey Falls was augmented with a painted mountainscape and a slight optical tilt-down to avoid the locked-off composition common in matte work. / Peering through the Vistavision plate camera, Evans determines the placement of a latent image matte line. / Though most matte shot environments are at least partially real, one view of the Nelwyns crossing a seemingly treacherous gorge was totally fabricated. / The only live elements in the shot were the character silhouettes - accomplished simply by having the actors parade across the top of a truck. CINEFEX 35 • 39 SKE"],["40","Willow","outside Queen Baumorda's castle were photographed in front of a full-scale facade built on a desolate location Though huge in itself, the castle set represented only a department where they were Paul Huston adds finishing castle. When finished, the backdrop - through a scrim enhanced with additional painted detail in a technique inherent in both miniatures Live-action sequences in Wales by production designer Allan Cameron. small section of the full structure. Consequently, establishing shots were relegated to the matte accomplished through a unique combination of miniatures and matte paintings. / Modelmaker touches to his miniature version of the Nockmaar citadel was shot latent image against a painted to produce diffusion and aerial density - then that capitalized on the considerable strengths and matte paintings. 40 - CINEFEX 35 iature section of the wall and then flickered lights on it as if coming from a fire down at the bottom. The wall was a cone shape - three feet tall by two feet wide - with a radius of curvature about ten inches at the narrow end and two feet at the wide-open end. It was just stone relief in styrofoam, but it looked great with the lights flickering up the side. With a painting there is just no way to get the little shapes and lighting effects that you can get on the real thing.\" As a final touch, Huston made a cage with a skeleton in it which was hung on the wall. The matte department was also heavily involved in another castle interior - Bavmorda's expansive throneroom. \"Unfortunately,\" said Evans, \"the plate that was shot for us was unusable because of the lighting - it was just too dark and smoky to be rear-projected. So Sean Joyce worked out a new design with George and we reshot the plate here in latent image Vistavision on the main stage. Jeff Olsen and some other guys built a section of the stone floor in styrofoam and then we lit it to match the set in England. Caroleen Green did the painting, which was shot with the window areas backlit. The only thing that was live-action in the whole shot was the walkway leading up to the throne. I think George was pretty excited about it, as we were. It looks like a million dollar set, and yet it was done at ILM in just a couple of weeks at a very reasonable cost.\" While trying to find their way to Tir Asleen, Willow and Madmartigan find themselves in a huge canyon maze. To establish the setting, a shot was planned that would begin close in on the actors and then boom up into a down-angle revealing the full extent of the maze. Since nothing but a plate of the actors on horseback against some rocks actually existed, the matte department was called upon to supply the dramatic pay-off of the shot. \"Originally we thought it would just be a matter of doing a huge matte painting and then pulling back on it,\" Evans stated, \"but doing it that way would have created no parallax shift, which would have been a dead giveaway that it was flat artwork. The only way to do it convincingly was to take the Vistavision plate that had been shot for us and put it into a big To that end, Paul Huston sculpted an eighteen-by-twentyfive-foot section of the maze out of urethane foam. Seamlessly incorporated into one of the miniature sections was a two-footwide flat wall area set between two adjoining buttes. \"That flat section was glass,\" Huston explained, \"and the entire set directly behind it was hollow so that the plate could be projected onto the back of the glass. We set the miniature up into four main planes with things in between each so that it looked like all of the various parts of the shot were in the right places. The scene was photographed with a 100mm lens which tended to flatten everything out so there was no apparent perspective change as the camera boomed upward.\" Wade Childress and assistant Catherine Craig were responsible for shooting the canyon maze on the motion control Automatte camera - a task that required a major extension of its existing track. \"The basic method has been used before,\" Evans conceded. \"Even King Kong had a number of shots where plates were rear projected into miniatures. But it has never been done in color with a matched move and a big pull-back.\" Though the Tir Asleen castle was primarily shot either on location or with full-scale set pieces, a final scene of Willow's triumphant departure did require some matte work. A liveaction plate had been shot of a waving, cheering crowd in front of the castle gate; but when the gathering was later deemed too small for such a jubilant moment, the matte department was asked to fill it out with four additional crowd sections shot against bluescreen and matted into a Michael Pangrazio painting. Also contributing to the scene were banners and flags flying atop miniature castle towers built by Wesley Seeds. \"Wesley did a beautiful job of researching the banners,\" Huston noted. \"The ones we used were made from velour and looked like old tapestry banners. We also made some lightweight chenille flags and turned a fan on them. So we had big banners undulating slowly and little flags flapping in the wind.\" As originally conceived, the final scene in the film was to be a simple pull-back shot on the Nelwyn village as friends and family welcome Willow home. In editing, however, it was decided that the scope of the shot was too limited. \"George wanted to be able to keep pulling back,\" stated Evans, \"which was impossible to do with the existing footage since it had been shot in squeezed four-perf and was very smoky. So I designed a new shot that could be cut into the live-action and provide a huge panoramic view of the Nelwyn's land. George asked us to give it a sort of romantic feel, similar to the Hudson River school of painting.\" Through careful planning, Evans was able to design an expansive shot whose plate could be photographed with just a few costumed extras in an open field behind ILM. Everything else - vegetation and huts - would be added in the painting. \"Craig Barron supervised the photography of the plate from atop a twenty-five-foot platform. Little people, schoolchildren, pigs, goats, ponies and a couple of electric windmills were brought in as the core of the village. The rear-projection compositing, with a programmed pull-back and credit roll, was handled by Wade Childress. It was a very ambitious shot and it lasts on the screen for a whole minute while the credits roll over it - which I believe makes it the longest daylight matte shot ever done.\" Among the earliest preproduction concerns was how to effectively and economically incorporate one or more nine-inchtall brownies into nearly every major sequence in the film. It was evident to Dennis Muren that the best possible results would be obtained by using full-size actors in forced perspective settings - a technique employed extensively in Darby O'Gill and the Little People and on a smaller scale in Innerspace. However, the number of brownie shots and the range of locations effectively rendered the approach infeasible from a budget standpoint. Some sort of matting process would have to suffice. \"In one of our earliest meetings,\" said Muren, \"Ron voiced his"],["41","Willow","concern over how much freedom he would have with the camera in directing the live-action for the brownie sequences. Would he be able to dolly the camera around or would he have to limit its movement? My first reaction was to advise that he limit his camera movement. By the end of our meeting, however, I had figured out that it could be done with elaborate pin-block rephotography which would allow him to move the camera however he wanted. As long as we made special plates in Vistavision with the right contrast range on them and the right exposure, he could direct the sequences just like normal liveaction and the brownies could be plopped down into the backgrounds really well.\" Pin-blocking - first employed by ILM on the aerial tauntaun shots in The Empire Strikes Back - has in recent years become a standard tool at the facility. The technique essentially entails photographing a live-action plate - in the case of Willow, one or more brownies full-frame against blue - and then directly rephotographing that footage backlit on a motion control animation stand, thus enabling the subject image to be reduced in size and repositioned anywhere in the frame. By carefully plotting the camera movement in a given background plate, the pin-block operator can correspondingly match-move his bluescreen subject and fit it seamlessly into any part of the frame. Since perfectly executed background plates were essential to the pin-blocking process, Mike McAlister and a Vistavision camera crew spent five months working side by side with the main unit on locations in England and New Zealand. When principal when principal photography wrapped, McAlister then collaborated with Ron Howard and George Lucas on the selection of plates that would actually be used in the film. Once those were determined, he spent the next hectic month with effects cameraman Kim Marks and stage manager Ed Hirsch preparing for the bluescreen shoot. \"At that point there were 172 brownie shots designated to be in the movie,\" said McAlister. \"We located all the backgrounds and studied them and analyzed the lighting and the composition and the angle at which the background had been photographed. Then we made very detailed notes for each shot so that we wouldn't have to figure it out on the set.\" A prime concern was maintaining the proper scale for the brownies from shot to shot. \"A lot of things come into play when determining scale - we couldn't simply rely on a mathematical formula all the time. On most of the shots in England, we photographed a rehearsal or a separate take that had an actual-size brownie doll in the scene to use as a reference for lighting and scale. That worked for most of the shots, but sometimes the brownies wound up in different parts of the scene from what we had planned. When that happened we had to rely on the old eyeball technique, just taking various clues from the backgrounds to make sure we had the scale right.\" Because of the expansiveness and high-angle nature of most of the brownie shots, the rearlit fluorescent bluescreens employed routinely at the facility were inadequate to the task of surrounding the figures completely with blue. \"Some of the CINEFEX 35 • 41"],["42","Willow","42 - CINEFEX 35"],["43","Willow","brownie shots we wound up doing with oversized sets,\" said Muren, \"but in order to get mattes for the down-shots, we went to a deserted Ford plant that we've used a number of times and set up a blue cyclorama about twenty-five high that swept down onto a painted blue floor seventy-feet square. Essentially what we had was a huge video-style chroma-key stage - all prelit - and that was our set. Using this approach, we could get any angle we wanted - straight down, crawling on the ground, whatever. The brownies could be running, they could jump up on things and jump off. Of course we would much rather have done the shots with the best quality bluescreen possible, but under the circumstances I think the frontlit approach worked quite well. Basically it was a matter of doing somewhat sloppy quality work on the front end - with the advantage of being able to do fifteen or twenty setups a day - and then working it out at the back end so that it matched the rest of the work. In a show like this, there was really no alternative.\" McAlister spent much of his one-month preparation time devising ways for the bluescreen performers to interact convincingly with people and props and set pieces present only on the background plate. \"I thought that the best way to accomplish the interaction would be to construct a bluescreen set that was a facsimile of the actual set on location. So if there was a little hill that the brownies were supposed to run up on, then we would actually have a little hill built on our bluescreen set. That way the body English would all be the same - the brownies would be leaning into the hill when they were running up or leaning away from it when they were running down. Or if there was a log in the scene, we would actually put something there on our set for them to jump over. We hoped that if we had a frontlit bluescreen and all these various shapes painted blue, the brownies would fit right in with the live-action.\" Since even the king-size bluescreen stage was not large enough for many of the walking and running shots, a treadmill was incorporated into the set - though working with it tended to be somewhat confusing. \"At first there was the question of how many footsteps a brownie had to take to cover one foot of human-size space. I went out in the parking lot and walked for one hundred feet and counted how many steps it took. Then I ran for one hundred feet and counted how many steps that took. Using those numbers as a basis, I was able to determine how many steps a brownie would have to take to cover a given distance. That was a major factor in directing the brownies on the bluescreen set because sometimes the shots required them to hit specific marks at specific times - or the shot might require that the brownie run fifteen feet. Well fifteen feet meant nothing to us because the actor was running on a treadmill - so we would break that down in terms of how long it would take a brownie to run fifteen feet and just cue him accordingly. On the set, Ron deferred all of that technical stuff to me - he really didn't want to get involved with it. He was more concerned with getting his character performances. Kim Marks and Ed Hirsch were invaluable to me during this whole process. In fact, they did most of the actual hands-on work during shooting while I was preparing for the next setup. That way we were able to keep the flow going from one shot to the next without too much down time in between. We were able to average about ten to twelve setups a day - which when dealing with bluescreen is a phenomenal feat.\" Once the bluescreen session was finished, McAlister had to decide - shot by shot - which technique would best fit the little characters into each scene. Though pin-blocking was used predominantly, for some shots the approach was either awkward or unnecessary. \"The general rule was that anytime the brownie was smaller than half the height of the frame, we shot it as a pin-block element. If it was larger than half the height of the frame, we would not be able to get enough reduction for the pin-blocking to be effective. I figured that a two-to-one reduction was minumum in order to avoid grain build-up, because pin-blocking requires that we go another generation before we get a compositing element. The other factor in determining whether or not a shot should be pin-blocked was what sort of interaction there was with the set pieces or whether the camera was moving. We had some really long shots where the brownies were very small in the frame that we didn't pin-block at all — there was no movement on the background and the perspective was simple, so we just gave them a start and stop mark and made sure the angle and height of the camera were correct.\" In most cases, however, the camera was moving, so the footage was turned over to animator John Knoll and his pinblock mechanism. \"These types of effects have been done with standard bluescreen before,\" said Knoll, \"but the problem has always been matching up the motion correctly. Very few of the brownie shots could have been done one-to-one on the stage because there was too much movement in them. For instance, in the scene where Willow holds Rool in his fingers, we had bluescreen footage with Rool flailing his arms and body around and that had to match up exactly to where Willow's fingertips were the whole time. So most of these things really involved two matched moves - one for the background plate and one for the bluescreen material - and I would have to plot them both and mathematically combine the two moves so the elements would work together. When the bluescreen material was shot on stage, the object was to keep the brownies as large in the frame as possible to insure the best reproduction when it was rephotographed. As a result, there was generally some panning and tilting that had to be subtracted out. Also, on the treadmill shots I would have to remove the treadmill motion. Usually it took about half a day to match-move the background plate and half a day to match-move the bluescreen plate. Then it took most of a second day to fit the two together. After that I'd shoot a test and optical would do a temporary black-andwhite composite that would be cut into the film to see if it was working. If it was, we would then proceed to the next step and shoot color. Most of the scenes also involved roto work. Whenever a brownie went behind something in the plate - a hand The sinister Nockmaar castle as revealed in full through the combined miniature and matte painting technique. / Matte paintings were also featured within the castle. Aside from the dais and the walkway, the entirety of Baumorda's gloomy throneroom was a latent image matte painting. Since the painting itself was rendered on glass - by Caroleen Green - the window areas were left transparent so they could be heavily backlit to produce a convincing flare effect. / Originally it had been planned to do the shot with a rear-projected live-action element photographed in England. It was not until the main unit wrapped, however, that the plate was found to be inadequately lit. To remedy the problem, a styrofoam walkway and a mockup throne were quickly constructed on the main ILM stage and the live-action was reaccomplished using standins for the principal players. CINEFEX 35 • 43"],["44","Willow","While traversing a dark and mystical forest, Willow and Meegosh are attacked and nine-inch-tall brownies - two of whom later join their party. Because of the vast number of brownie shots, most were accomplished by bluescreen characters into live-action plates. In some instances, however, oversize Visual effects cosupervisor camera assistant Pat McArdle brownies Rool and Franiean runaway cart. / On location in the Muir Woods - where the forest scenes were shot - production executive lan Warwick Davis' chest while reference shot. Such shots were produced for all the brownie scenes to provide necessary information for scaling and positioning the tiny bluescreen characters effects cosupervisor Dennis Muren studies a live-action plate and a corresponding grid pattern with desired brownie placements noted. overcome by a swarm of optical insertion of sets were constructed. Michael McAlister and prepare for a shot of being joustled about an enlarged section of a Bryce places a cardboard cutout of a brownie on effects cameraman Scott Farrar prepares for a those later tasked with into each frame. / Visual 44 * CINEFEX 35 or a piece of furniture, for example - the roto department would have to go in and create articulate mattes. Often there were quite a few, and some of them were really complicated.\" Though the frontlit bluescreen proved expedient in producing large numbers of brownie elements, the resulting material proved troublesome for optical cosupervisor Kenneth Smith. \"There are some very good reasons why painted blue is not considered the industry standard anymore. The quality of the blue is far inferior to the illuminated bluescreens we normally use and we had to worry about things like ambient reflection from the blue light on feet and legs, With a painted blue floor, the main drawback in terms of fitting the brownies into a shot is that the cameramen are hampered in how well they can light the brownies to fit into a specific background. For the most part, the lighting has to be flat throughout. Consequently, if the brownies had to move from light to dark in the scene, we would have to do that lighting effect in optical. If they had tried to do it on the stage, there would have been no matte - the has tag be bluescreen has to be fully illuminated. So we had to match it up to the background and create all the lights and darks as they walked around the frame. Plus the matting was very tough - especially since one of the brownies had really frizzy hair. I am sure whoever was minding the cash register looked at the real advantage of the time savings and the number of shots you could do on a painted blue floor and decided that it was a cost effective way to go. But it took so much more time in optical that any money saved at the beginning of the production was very possibly spent in the amount of time it took to generate the elements at our end of the process.\" In addition to pin-blocking the brownies, the animation department provided simulated shadows to help anchor the bluescreen figures into their live-action settings. \"Brownie shadows were a real problem on this show,\" noted animation supervisor Wes Takahashi. \"In much of the forest sequence there were twenty or more brownies in every shot, and to do traditional hand animation for each of them would have taken forever - especially since just one character's shadow for an average shot can take up to a week, not including ink and paint time. So we attacked each shot with not only hand animation but also by taking artwork that was just the generalized shape of a brownie character and match-moving that artwork under the motion control camera so that it would fit the movements of these characters as they were interacting in the scene. If the shadows in the original background plate were really diffused we could get away with just a hint of a shadow in the scene, as long as it was moving with the character. Ultimately, that approach was refined even further by cameraman Charlie Clavadetscher who segmented the generalized brownie shape and created a separate program for each body part. The results were very lifelike and enabled us to knock weeks off our schedule.\" One particularly difficult shot required yet another technique. \"There were about seventeen brownies standing around.\" Knoll recalled, \"just shifting a little bit without really moving. Using hand animation for the shadows in that shot would have been very difficult because they were very tiny - and after going through all the steps of being inked and painted and photographed, they would probably have had an unsteady edge. So for that shot I got optical to make clear center intermattes for me. I took those and projected them down onto a card and lined them up so that the perspective was correct, then photographed the projected elements onto high-con stock and lined them up with the feet of the characters so the shadows would be in the right place. It was a little tricky getting the right projecting angle for proper perspective - but once everything was lined up correctly, I was able to knock out a whole bunch of them very quickly.\" Animation also supplied tiny arrows for a sequence in which the brownies launch an assault on Willow and Meegosh. \"Since no one wanted to contend with arrows putting out expensive lights and destroying bluescreens,\" said Takahashi, \"all the brownies were shot just pantomiming the act of pulling arrows out of their quivers and firing. So we were responsible for animating the arrows. The model shop came up with some small-scale arrows - about two inches long - and we put them on model movers underneath our animation cameras. Not only did they have to fly with a lot of blur, but they also had to look like they were being drawn out of quivers and placed in bows. Bruce Walters had to program all that, as well as determine the specific trajectory for each arrow. And sometimes, not only were the brownies pulling arrows out of their quivers and firing, they were also running down branches and logs at the same time. So it was quite a feat.\" The brownies' attack is quelled by the appearance of Cherlindrea - whose ethereal image was created in part by photographing actress Maria Holvoe against black with a fog filter. \"She wore very lightweight silk clothes that billowed about when we put wind on her,\" recalled Muren. \"We shot her footage at high speed and overexposed it about four stops. We also did a lot of enhancements in optical to add trailing edges to her and make her look somewhat gaseous - although she was so beautiful and her performance was so perfect that Ron and George didn't want to interfere too much with a lot of effects.\" Though an attempt had been made to shoot background forest plates on location, the demands of interactive lighting produced by Cherlindrea's presence proved to be too cumbersome. Consequently, model shop supervisor Lorne Peterson was commissioned to create a miniature third-scale forest for the sequence. \"Having already done redwood forests for a number of other projects, we were beginning to feel like experts - we definitely knew the territory. Fortunately we had saved everything from those earlier shows - mainly because those trees are so expensive to build - but it still took a lot of man-hours to get them up to snuff and modify them for the scale we were working in. We took trees left over from Jedi that were about seven to eight inches in diameter and four feet tall and used those for our small to medium-sized trees. The little inch-and-"],["45","Willow","CINEFEX 35 • 45"],["46","Willow","46 - CINEFEX 35"],["47","Willow","a-half diameter trees from E.T. became saplings. Dennis also wanted some big stumps - about two feet in diameter - so Rick Anderson and Chuck Wiley and I each carved one out of green foam. And because there was going to be a lot of backlighting when Cherlindrea came through the forest, we stripped a lot of bark off real redwood trees and then glued the fibers onto our own trees to create that odd kind of fuzzy look that redwoods have. For branches we used juniper - some of which was still left over from E.T. The stuff will last for years. Even if it starts to yellow a bit, all we have to do is touch it up with some spray paint, We also used dead branches and some burnt sections of trunk - anything to give it a little extra character. And, of course, there was a lot of work preparing just the ground cover and little shrubs and all the other things that had to be there besides the actual trees. With all the saplings and everything, the miniature had over one hundred trees in it.\" Six people working full-time managed to complete the set in about three-and-a-half weeks. When finished, it covered an area eighteen feet wide by twelve feet deep and had an overall height of approximately ten feet. \"Dennis wanted to make sure the tops of the trees were there to eliminate any chance of terminated shadows on the ground. On Jedi we used camouflage netting on top of our forest set - and if you look closely, you can see that camouflage pattern in the shadows. To avoid that on this film, we used tree branches in front of the lights to create a natural pattern. Another improvement was putting spikes on the bottoms of all the trees. The whole forest was built on a bed of foam; so anytime we wanted to move a tree, all we had to do was just pull it out and stick it somewhere else. In forest shots in the past, Dennis would have to pick and choose his camera angles very carefully; but in this case he was able to do anything he wanted, because any tree at all could be moved about and repositioned or totally eliminated in favor of another.\" Shooting the miniature forest took about a month. To create the interactive illumination emanating from Cherlindrea, a stage light was attached to a twenty-foot boom arm mounted on a motion control track for maximum steadiness. Since the camera was shooting at one frame per second, the boom had to move very slowly and smoothly. Smoke was pumped into the set to add a sense of eeriness. Cherlindrea's materialization - and later dematerialization - was an effect worked out by John Knoll. \"I rear-projected a moving plate of Cherlindrea,\" Knoll explained, \"and took two pieces of ripple glass that were on motorized movers. Starting with the ripple glass down in contact with the screen, the image was fairly undistorted and recognizable. But as the shot progressed, I lifted the glass away from the image and it started to get more and more fragmented and distorted. The two pieces of ripple glass were moving at slightly different speeds, and one of them was on a north-south as well as z-axis; so although both pieces of glass were moving towards camera, one of them was moving up as well. That produced some crawl between the images, which gave it a nice motion. The only difficulty with The predominant shrink-tofit process used on the brownies was pin-blocking - a technique which entailed shooting brownie actors full-frame against bluescreen and then rephotographing that footage on an animation stand in proper scale and position and with appropriate camera moves plotted from the background plate. / To create a more expansive bluescreen environment than the ones on hand at ILM, a vacant auto plant was converted into a frontlit bluescreen stage for the brownie shoot. Even this, however, was too small for many of the walking and running scenes; so a treadmill was incorporated into some of the setups. / Willow comes face-to-face with a forest fairy. / Dennis Muren makes adjustments to the gown being worn by actress Maria Holvoe as the fairy queen Cherlindrea, Backlighting coupled with heavy diffusion enhanced her ethereal presence. / When the apparatus that would have provided interactive lighting from Cherlindrea proved too cumbersome on location, chief modelmaker Lorne Peterson was commissioned to replicate the forest in one-third scale. CINEFEX 35 • 47"],["48","Willow","maze. As conceived, the shot was to begin on the riders and then pull up and back to reveal the full expanse of the setting. Since the maze did not exist in reality, the would not have produced credibility, so a miniature constructed in foam by Paul Huston and backed up by a Automatte cameraman Wade Catherine Craig prepare to shoot the miniature setting. / Incorporated into the set was a rear-process screen onto which the live-action plate element was projected during a separate motion control pass. Working from a frame enlargement of the plate, Chris Evans blends the Fleeing enemy pursuers, Willow and Madmartigan seek refuge in a canyon matte department was engaged to create one. A straight matte painting the perspective shifts required to insure canyon maze was painting of the distant horizon and sky. / Childress and assistant screen area into the miniature while Huston attends to last-minute details in the rear. 48 - CINEFEX 35 the technique was controlling it. If it was kept too much in focus, it looked exactly like what it was - a rear-projected image through ripple glass. If it was too soft, it just looked outof-focus and globby. Striking that balance was the hardest part. I lined it up very closely to the original element so that as the glass got closer and closer, right at the very end - before it actually touched - we did a quick optical dissolve to the real Ripple glass was also employed by Bruce Walters to provide light effects on shots of Willow as Cherlindrea addresses him. \"Bruce came up with this aura effect that really added a lot of texture to the shots and made it look as though something magical was happening off-screen,\" Wes Takahashi commented \"He did it by projecting lights through motion controlled ripple glass onto a surface, diffusing the heck out of it and shooting it. When finally composited into the shot, gaseous light shards appeared to be emanating from an off-screen source.\" The animation department also provided wings for the tiny fairies that surround Cherlindrea, although the fairies themselves were actually gymnasts photographed on wire rigs against bluescreen. \"From the beginning,\" Takashashi continued, \"when we were first conceptualizing what the little fairies would look like, it seemed that the wings were going to pose a problem. Dennis thought that they would probably have to be animated, but I thought that was impractical since there were so many of them. Eventually we ended up creating model wings that could be manipulated. I constructed the wings by cutting them out of pieces of clear plastic and etching in little membranes and then covering them with a diffraction grading film that creates kind of a spectrum of light as light hits its surface. The wings were about three inches high and looked like dragonfly wings. Ron Howard asked that they have ragged, wispy edges to make them look more feminine, so the design was modified to accommodate that. Then John Knoll created an incredible machine that would flap the wings independently using motion control. Bruce Walters took over the next step by putting this wing flapper into a huge rig that could tilt and roll and move in just about any axis. Then he created all the motion control programs that kept these wings aligned to the fairies - which had already been shot on stage. Although the gymnasts playing the parts were going through their acting acrobatics, the shots themselves had no programmed moves so the fairies remained in the middle of the frame. We were thus able to take that film and program our wings to the movements of the gymnasts at one-to-one. Then Peter Daulton pin-blocked each fairy and corresponding set of wings. He had turned one of the track cameras into a makeshift animation camera and was responsible for programming the flight paths of the fairies and their wings. Rotomattes were later produced for frames in which body parts obstructed the wings. Peter also added streak passes to the flight paths so that there was a little streak trailing each fairy. In some of the closeup shots there was fairy dust trailing off also - Gordon Baker animated that. In one shot a fairy comes in and kisses willow on the nose, so we added some intereactive light to his face by just programming a light back in animation and having its movements coordinate to the actions of the fairy. Tom Bertino then did a matte so that the light would appear only on Willow and not on the background.\" Each department's contribution to the Cherlindrea sequence came together in optical under Ken Smith's supervision. \"Our main problem with the fairy forest sequence was the fact that Cherlindrea had been shot against black with heavy diffusion. It looked great in the dailies, of course - she looked much more magical with heavy fog on her than she did without it — and the original plan was to simply double-expose her into the scene without having to worry about a matte. But then when the miniature forest footage was shot, they put this big light rig into the set with a black card over it to prevent lens flares. That was necessary because if there was a lens flare on the element, then Cherlindrea would be over the lens flare and that would destroy the illusion that the light was coming from her. But with this black card in the frame, we could no longer DX her into the shot. We had to matte her over the card or she would have this big geometrical shape right in the middle of her. With all the diffusion on her, that was really difficult.\" At Cherlindrea's bidding, Willow sets off in search of the sorceress Fin Raziel whom he is told can help him get Elora Danan safely to Tir Asleen. When he finally locates her - stranded on a small island - he finds to his dismay that she has been transformed into a possum by Bavmorda. With a magic wand he has been given by the fairy queen, Willow attempts to restore the sorceress to her human form but succeeds only in changing her from one animal into another. From an effects point of view, one of the most lingering and perplexing problems was how to convincingly portray Raziel as a talking character in her various animal forms. Given her importance and dignity, it was essential that a corny 'Francis the Talking Mule' type approach be avoided at all cost. Absolute realism was considered vital. Extensive footage of Raziel's animal forms - a possum, a goat and a bird - had been shot during principal photography to afford the editors ample choice in finding the right action and attitude to fit each bit of scripted dialogue. John Van Vliet - whose Available Light company in Burbank had produced impressive work for ILM in the past - was approached to take on the challenge of producing animated mouths for the live-action animal shots. \"When we first went up to ILM to look at their shots,\" Van Vliet recalled, \"they rolled a couple of plates for us and said, Well, what can you do with it?' They laughed, we laughed - there was nervous laughter all around because no one seemed to be very confident that this could be done without it looking ridiculous. The whole sequence had been shot on the assumption that it would work - but there were some backup ideas just in case. Some of it could have been done with puppets; and there was also the possibility of just having the voice there, as if the animal was projecting its thoughts telepathically. Frankly, we thought that after they"],["49","Willow","CINEFEX 35 • 49"],["50","Willow","50 - CINEFEX 35"],["51","Willow","saw a test showing what we could do with animation that they'd fall all over themselves rushing to the mind-melding idea or to guys with their hands stuck inside animal puppets. But we agreed to take it on - and as it turned out, no puppets were used at all.\" Basically, Available Light used a modified animation technique to create a semblance of speech in the live-action animals. \"We elected to go with a photo-roto approach rather than standard rotoscoping because we needed every nuance of angle we could get and to draw that kind of detail would have taken forever. We have a little mini-processing machine, so all we did to produce the photo-rotos was just take the workprint and project it down onto positive print paper. As far as the animation was concerned, we were only working within a small area of the frame - maybe one percent - so the bigger we could go, the better the detail was going to be when it was shrunk down. Consequently, we enlarged each frame to cover the entire top of the Oxberry bed - about forty inches across - but because the only thing we were actually concerned with was the mouth, we would then just go in and photo-roto a twelve-inch-wide area.\" While the photo-rotos were being made - some two thousand of them - Bill Arance produced a standard cartoonstyle sound reading, Working with a magnetic sound track provided for the shots they would be animating, he employed a squawk box and a synchronizer to transfer the animal dialogue first onto 35mm paper tape and then to count sheets the animators could use to cue their vocalizations. Once the photo-rotos and the sound readings were completed, the next step was the actual animation - done by John Van Vliet, Mike Lessa, Kathleen Quaife-Hodge and Daryl Rooney. Before the animation could begin, however, Van Vliet and his crew had some reseach to do. \"It was like taking a crash course in animal dentistry. We went to the library and tried to find pictures of possums, for instance, with their mouths open. We never did find the exact kind of possum that was in the film, but we got close enough. Then we came up with a model sheet for the mouth and we put a lot of detail into it. Next we took the workprint and studied it to decide what we wanted to do and where we wanted the nuances. Then we went in and did our breakdown. We did it on eights first - just going in and blocking it out real fast. If that seemed to be working and flowing well, we went back and did it on fours, cleaning it up a little bit as we went. Once we got on fours we started doing video tests and running the soundtrack with it. From there we just kept refining it until it worked perfectly - kind of like sculpting. One of the differences between doing this and standard cartoon animation was that there had to be a blur. In cartoon animation there is no blur factor between frames - but because it is a cartoon world, it reads fine and everybody buys it. We also found that we had to 'deanimate' stuff and take out a lot of the things that accentuated the animation too much. How much can an animal enunciate and make consonants and vowels without looking silly? We'd get into these raging discussions Willow finds an ally in Fin Raziel, a sorceress who has been transformed into a possum by Baumorda's magic. Hoping to convincingly portray the animal speaking lines of human dialogue, ILM enlisted the services of animator John Van Vliet and his Available Light company. / To accomplish the difficult lip-sync animation, blackand-white frame enlargements were first made for each frame of the live-action in which the possum was to speak. Using these for precise alignment reference, animators prepared standard animation pencil tests. Early in the process, Van Vliet roughs in the extremes. / Associate Katherine Kean shoots a move test on a video animation system to provide the animators with prompt feedback on work in progress. / Referring to a model sheet, Van Vliet adds color to the pencil renderings. / Cameraman Joseph Thomas then photographs a film test on the Oxberry animation stand. Once completed and approved, the animation was transferred by hand onto cels - using self-colored ink lines - then painted and photographed for final compositing into the mouth area of the animal. CINEFEX 35 • 51"],["52","Willow","arrive at Tir Asleen to find moments they are attacked headed fire-breathing dragon conjured up from the castle moat. Under visual effects cosupervisor Phil Tippett, two stop-motion puppets sequence - one depicting scale consisting only of the heads and necks. Using gomotion technology for the steps in between frames to accomplish the detail work on a scene calling for the creature to snatch one of the warriors and tear him apart. / Working with the partial cioseup. / Though most of the dragon elements were composited optically into Willow and Madmartigan the castle deserted - but not for long. Within by troops loyal to Queen Baumorda and by a twowere constructed for the the dragon in its entirety and another of the same major puppet moves, animator Tom St. Amand puppet, animator Harry Walton prepares for a shot bluescreen and live-action plates, rear projection and in-camera compositing were used wherever feasible for economy and speed. 52 * CINEFEX 35 about how much of an 'ooh' sound a possum can make. We didn't want it to look like Kermit the frog flapping his gums, yet it still had to have enough there so that it looked like the animal was talking. The level of detail was so precise that a pencil width's difference could make something look goofy.\" Rather than use xerox technology - now standard in cartoon animation to transfer fully refined drawings onto animation cels - the final rendering was done by hand using self-colored inks to eliminate black lines between the tones and colors that would have destroyed the illusion of reality. \"In standard cartoon animation today it is very unusual for people to hand-ink anything,\" Van Vliet commented. \"In fact, it is practically a lost art because almost everything is done with xerox. Xerox was out for us because it gives you a big honking black line and we were drawing beyond its resolution capabilities. So this was like going back to the old days.\" The laborious inking and painting process - accomplished by Katherine Kean, Byron Werner and Judith Bell - consisted of inking in only the color that was being painted at the time. \"In animation,\" said Kean, \"we always eat is paint on the back side of the cel so that the other side is perfectly smooth, without brush strokes or bumps on it. In this case we would have to go through the entire scene with just one color and then wait for that to dry. Then we would go back in and ink another line - and keep doing that over and over until it was done. Usually we wound up using seven or eight different colors, although at one point we had as many as fourteen.\" After the animation was completed, some final rotoscope work was needed to fit the artwork precisely into the designated mouth \"When we did the photography,\" said Van Vliet, \"we shot each frame toplit first and then pulled out the black paper behind it and shot it bottomlit. Later the two were separated out in the printer. That way we could be sure of a perfect matte. The standard Acme peg system is really tight, but if you take something off and then put it back down the registration can be off a half-a-hair - which we judged would be unacceptable for this stuff. With our approach, we could shoot the animation and the matte without ever having to move the cel because the ink itself was acting as the matte. We then softened the mattes a little bit so they would fit into the live-action.\" Final optical assembly was accomplished at Hollywood Optical Service and at Pacific Title. Essentially the same process was employed for the talking goat, although modifications were needed to allow for the different shape of its mouth and jaw. \"The problem with the goat was that he was chewing most of the time, and his mouth always seemed to be open when we needed it to be closed and closed when we needed it to be open. At first we tried to go in and remove his whole lower jaw so that we could drop in a matchmoved piece of goat fur and then animate under more controlled conditions. It was pretty complicated because the camera was pushing in on the goat during the shot and he was flopping his head all around. But we did a program that looked great - it pitched and rolled and the whole thing. Then we did the animation and put it together and it came back with this beautiful lip-synced mouth that looked astounding - but there was this growth of fur on the bottom of his head that looked like some kind of awful parasite. We could probably have made it work with the proper application of time and money - but in this case we had neither. So we went over our options, which were to continue to let the goat chew his cud and do the dialogue over it and live with it, or split the difference and take the lips that were there and just do the best we could - which is what we did. We could open the mouth a little bit - not as much as it should have been to make certain sounds, but more than it was originally. We just had to settle for a compromise with the goat that allowed him to talk while he chewed.\" Available Light was wrapping up final details on the project when on April Fool's Day a call came in from ILM. \"They told us they had three more shots for us, and we said, 'Yeah, this is a joke, right?' But they weren't joking and they sent down three bird shots which we ran through very quickly in about a weekand-a-half. In a way, these were easier since there were no lips on the bird. All we had to do was open and close the beak. But they were harder in that they were closeups. Also we ran into a problem because when the animated mouth of the bird was opened, the real beak was still behind it and we couldn't at first figure out what to do with it. We thought of taking the beak out and dropping something else in that looked like background air, but in the end we wound up being able to cheat the angles so that the beak just looked like the inside of the bird's mouth. We managed to get away with it, partly because there were some very good editorial choices.\" Ultimately, thirty-five talking animals shots were completed by Available Light in their five-month stint on the project. Even more fantastic than talking birds and possums was the two-headed fire-breathing dragon that emerges from the moat at Tir Asleen during a climactic confrontation between Madmartigan's rebel band and Bavmorda's powerful army led by General Kael. Dubbed the Eborsisk - a tongue-in-cheek nod to the critical team of Siskel and Ebert - the dragon fell under the auspices of Phil Tippett and his crew. Unlike other such creatures in movies past, Tippett wanted to attempt something that was not only unique in appearance but also had some biological rationale for its incendiary capabilities. \"George wanted something that had a lot of realistic elements in it,\" Tippett explained, 'but he wanted it to have a fanciful side as well. Initially he thought we ought to put fifteen heads on it, but we managed to talk him out of it. How do you make something with fifteen heads look realistic? Finally he agreed to two. At the time I was heavily immersed in Robocop, so Richard Van der Wende came up with a number of possibilities for the head design. Ultimately George tore up pieces of paper and we all voted on designs number one through five - and number five hit as the Eborsisk design most likely to succeed. Many times designs just seem to follow the standard of what a movie mon-"],["53","Willow","CINEFEX 35 • 53 ILM"],["54","Willow","54 * CINEFEX 35"],["55","Willow","ster should look like, but this one had a real offbeat quality. It was completely unfamiliar - kind of an elephant seal and shark combination.\" Since puppet construction began before the live-action sets were built, certain decisions had to be based solely on the screenplay. \"The script called for the Eborsisk to emerge out of a moat surrounding the castle. In my mind, a moat is a huge expanse of water full of crocodiles. When it was finally built, however, it ended up looking like a duck pond about twenty feet across. Since George had insisted that the dragon be a great, massive, powerful animal thirty feet tall, it became a design problem to figure out how to get that much dragon out of just a little bit of water. Ultimately, I worked out a design for the backside of the creature that was truncated perspectively - it diminished very quickly - so all that is really seen in the movie is the part that crawls out of the water. You never see his back legs at all and you only see his backside when he dies and his hump sticks up out of the pit.\" Using molds that were cast from a sculpture by Randy Dutra, two stop-motion puppets were built - one of the entire dragon body that was two-and-a-half feet long, and another in the same scale that included only the heads and shoulders. The armatures were designed by Tom St. Amand and built by Blair Clark. \"Duplicating a living thing is always very complicated,\" Tippett noted, \"because there are so many subtle things happening in real musculature - little nervous twitches and stuff like that. We couldn't do everything we would have liked to make the puppets realistic - it would have been too time-consuming and the Eborsisk wasn't really the central character in the sequence anyway - but we did do some very general things like having his skin wrinkle up and unfold and adding methane sacks that pulsated. We also paid a lot of attention to his eyes, making sure that they were always doing something that was more engaging and psychologically motivated than what you normally see in stop-motion.\" Two other stop-motion charac ters were also built by Blair Clark - a three-and-a-half-inch troll for a scene in which the dragon plucks one off a bridge, and a three-inch-tall Madmartigan for a sequence of shots in which the warrior leaps onto the Eborsisk's neck. Tom St. Amand and Harry Walton accomplished the animation. \"The puppets were shot using our go-motion computerized rod puppet technique,\" Tippett explained, \"with some supplementary hand animation. About three-quarters of the sequence •of the the ence was shot bluescreen and composited optically, while the other quarter was done with rear projection and in-camera compositing. We did that not only for economy and speed, but also as sort of an experiment to see how close we could get the rearprojection shots to look like the ones that were done in optical. As it turned out, they weren't very close at all. The bluescreen shots were very clean and there was much more of a sense of fluidity in them, mainly because of the exposure times. With the bluescreen stuff the exposures were no more than a couple of seconds long, so the animator could keep direct contact with the puppet. But with the rear-screen work - since the show was shot anamorphic and the lenses were terribly slow - some of the exposures were as long as three minutes. Harry and Tom did fine work, but it was very frustrating for them because in three minutes' time you lose contact with what you're doing. You're not even using the same muscles that you use if the exposure is only two seconds. Also, with the rear-screen work they were limited to rods that were underneath the table or somehow out of the shot, whereas in bluescreen the rods can be anywhere since part of the process is to roto them out anyway. All in all, the experiment turned into something of a nightmare. Since the two techniques did produce such different results, we ended up having to switch shots around so that we never cut directly from a bluescreen shot to a rear-projection shot - the differInitially, Tippett had hoped to play the two heads off against each other, but the limited screen time finally afforded the dragon did not provide sufficient opportunity for that kind of character development. \"We also found that most of what we tried to do to give the heads personality wound up being comical - which wasn't in keeping with the dragon's purpose. I had also wanted the sequence to play subjectively, with the dragon seen primarily from below as if the audience was a participant in the battle and the footage was being shot with a hand-held camera. But the sequence was shot in a much more understated manner and what remained was just enough camera movement to create difficulties. In some instances there were radical pans and tilts and for those we made low-con or special flash prints and put them up on a rear-projection screen. Harry Walton lit his own setups, while Tom St. Amand worked with Pete Kozachik as a unit. In his down time, Pete would plot the various elements such as fire and water which had to match either moving scenes or moving dragon heads.\" The fire-breathing effects in Dragonslayer had required extensive hand-articulated rotomatting for the flame elements - separately photographed - to appear as though they were originating from within the dragon's mouth. Hoping to create a different and simpler effect for the Eborsisk, Tippett opted for a butane torch look - maintaining a slight distance between the dragon's mouth and the actual flame. \"We fitted the dragon heads with methane sacks that had air-controlled bladders in them. When we pumped up the bladders, the methane sacks under their throats would expand as the dragon inhaled. Then with the exhale, the sacks would collapse as the gas came out. Each tongue had flint strikers on its tip so that when the monster reared back, his methane sack would inflate, his mouth would open and the two parts of his tongue would strike and ignite a spark that was followed by the gas.\" The actual flame elements - engineered by pyrotechnician Bob Finley - were shot against black and composited separately, \"The flames were shot static because they were twenty to thirty feet long and the kind of apparatus that it takes to generate that size flame was much too bulky to move around. Pat Sweeney shot Effects cameraman Peter Kozachik - working with Tom St. Amand - lines up a low-angle bluescreen shot. / During the battle, Madmartigan leaps onto one of the dragon's heads and pins its mouth shut with his sword. Unable to discharge its fiery blast, the creature thrashes about frantically and then explodes. For the selfdestruction scene, Phil Tippett loads a larger scale head with dragon 'innards' which were then laced with pyrotechnics provided by Bob Finley. The exploding head was shot high-speed and then intercut with gomotion puppet photography. / The dragon's fiery breath was shot as a separate element using a powerful flame-thrower mounted on a forklift in the ILM parking lot. For shots of the flame coming directly toward camera, cameraman Patrick Sweeney was shielded inside a protective booth. CINEFEX 35 • 55"],["56","Willow","With a magic wand given to him by Cherlindrea, Willow at last manages to restore Raziel to her human form - but only after propelling her through a quick succession of other creatures en route. transformation - from goat to ostrich to turtle to tiger uninterrupted flow, Dennis puppets was used - with them achieved via computer animation. The first puppet designer Tad Krzanowski. / ostrich commences, horns and ears are withdrawn and stretch. / Fully extended, the pressure which draws the outer features down onto an Puppeteering coordinator David Allen discusses the next-stage ostrich puppet Wilkins. / Peacock feathers fan out behind the ostrich To accomplish the to woman - in an Muren enlisted the full resources of the creature shop and the computer graphics department. A series of animatronic the transitions between was a third-scale goat created by mechanics As the transformation to the puppet begins to air-filled goat head is subjected to vacuum inner ostrich skull. / with modelmaker Jeffrey as operators manipulate rods and cables. 56 * CINEFEX 35 all of the flame elements - and it was really terrifying because in some instances these twenty-foot flames were shooting straight towards the camera. Pat was behind a glass enclosure for protection, but the first time they tested the flame he let out a bloodcurdling scream - which fortunately turned out to be a scream of terror rather than a scream of pain.\" In the end, the Eborsisk self-destructs when Madmartigan - astride one of the heads - drives his sword through the monster's muzzle, pinning its mouth shut. Unable to vent its fiery venom, the methane sack expands with internal pressure and finally explodes. \"We built a large Eborsisk head - about twoand-a-half feet long - and loaded it full of pyro material. Then we blew it up and shot it high-speed. The exploding head was carefully matched to a stop-motion shot that Harry Walton did of the monster rearing back, inflating its sack and starting to sneeze. We cut directly from the stop-motion head to the highspeed head when the explosion starts - and everything happens so quickly, it looks like it's part of the same cut.\" Optical compositing for the sequence was supervised by Bruce Nicholson. \"The main problem we had to deal with was the fact that the live-action material was shot with smoke. The Vistavision plates and the Eborsisk were shot without smoke because we were dealing with matted elements. As a result, we had to simulate in optical what was happening with smoke in the live-action. It became fairly complicated because the smoke kept changing from shot to shot in the live-action depending on wind conditions or how much smoke they had used. Finally we worked out an approach that involved flashing the scenes and/or using smoke elements that were shot at ILM against black. We put layers of flash into the scenes - foreground, midground and background - varying the levels depending on the cut. Normally, the monster would be in the foreground and we would have a certain percentage of flash over him and then a certain percentage behind. There were some shots where matte paintings were used to fill in the background and we would have to flash those areas as well. Then a smoke element might be put in, which would also be layered. But it was always determined cut by cut.\" Flashing also helped to blend the mismatched rear-projection and bluescreen shots by desaturating Despite the rebel victory over the two-headed dragon, trolls and even General Kael's army, little Elora Danan is captured and taken to Nockmaar where Bavmorda waits to perform her ritualistic killing. Intent on rescuing the child, Willow and Madmartigan lead their small band to the dark castle where Willow at last manages to restore Fin Raziel to her human form - but only after changing her through a rapid succession of other forms as well. The multistage transformation was a rare case in which the special effects unit was given almost complete creative control. \"George was open to Raziel changing into anything,\" said Dennis Muren, \"just as long as the transformation was amazing. He and Ron wanted definite size changes, but our hands were pretty much free as to what the character would"],["57","Willow","CINFFEX 35 • 57"],["58","Willow","actually become as long as she started as a goat and ended as a woman. We were determined to do it without cheating the audience - we wanted them to see the entire transformation, which meant lingering on it and not doing any cutaways.\" Given these requirements, Muren considered which effects technique would best serve the sequence. \"Right up to the end I was debating whether to go with traditional bladders and rubber and things like that or try to do something completely computer generated. The second option was appealing - but it was really beyond us, because so far there are no good software programs that are time-efficient. It would have taken months just to get up to speed and then several months more to do the animation. So we came up with a hybrid approach using a combination of models and the digital printer. I thought that if we designed it right, we would get a transformation that nobody had seen before using something that is a lot closer to being practical than a fully computer-generated human figure would be. Our first idea was to go from the goat to a deer because the faces are similar, but George said that wasn't different enough. So I got to thinking that maybe what would make this work would be to take advantage of the computer's ability to do things that cannot be done in any other way — a big scale change, for instance, going from something really small to something really big. So at that point we literally went for opposites - from a goat to an ostrich to a turtle to a tiger to a woman. We played up the differences rather than trying to go from one thing to something else that was similar.\" Muren's hybrid approach called for the creature shop to build a series of puppets capable of providing a real-image anchor to which the computer graphics could be tied. Each puppet would have mechanical abilities that allowed it to perform the first part of the tranformation into the next creature. By scanning the final stage of one puppet and the first stage of the one following it, the computer graphics department would then be able to digitally blend the transition between the two. The first transition - from goat into ostrich - was largely achieved with an ingenious half-scale rod puppet realized by Tad Krzanowski. \"The goat puppet was cast in foam and had stop-motion armatures inside of it and a fiberglass core to keep its shape. The whole front third of the body - from the base of the neck to a point behind the front legs - was designed so that when the armatured legs were moved in the proper way, they would form into something resembling a wing shape. Gordon Tate did an excellent job of sculpting it so that when its legs were folded down it looked like a goat, but when its legs were shifted up it looked like an ostrich. The neck was a very difficult part of the puppet because it had to expand to three times its length to become the ostrich neck. We used a special kind of foam for it that had a telescopic action - an eighteen-inch piece of foam that could be compressed to an inch in thickness and held by flexible cable. When the cable was released, the neck expanded and was helped along by a rod attached to the side of the head. The foam was covered with actual goat hair - applied 58 * CINEFEX 35"],["59","Willow","by Jean Bolte - and we had to do practically no makeup when the neck was extended to the ostrich length because the fur behaved just right.\" The head of the goat was made from airtight latex rubber and equipped internally with vacuum hoses. When an external vacuum generator was turned on, the latex rubber would collapse and fit tightly over a smaller ostrich head hidden within. \"The head was a sealed unit that had to be constructed especially to serve the vacuum process. The ostrich skull was hollow fiberglass with lots of undercutting to accommodate the extra latex rubber that would be drawn over it. To change the eyes from the goat to the ostrich was fairly simple. The goat eyes were actually quite large, even though they looked small, because the lids were half-closed and there was a bony ridge that protruded over them. When we vacuumed to the ostrich head, the ridge pulled back and we opened the eyes up fully which made them look very big. At the same time, we pulled the goat's antlers and ears slowly into the head with cables so they looked like they were disappearing.\" David Allen was brought in to oversee the articulation effort. While Tad Krzanowski manned the vacuum mechanism, head puppeteer Tony Hudson and six others operated the rod and cable functions. The entire transformation took about six seconds to achieve and was photographed against a bluescreen. \"Originally there had been talk of using go-motion on the transformation puppets,\" said Allen. \"The theory was that if they were all done in go-motion, then we could match exactly the timings of one model to the next and thereby help the computer animation system make an orderly transition from one to another. Everything would be in the place it should be relative to the overall action. But that idea was scrapped because it would have been so difficult mechanically - and more importantly, too time-consuming. Also there was some problem with the availability of a motion control apparatus. So it was decided that we would sort of freestyle everything and try to do these actions in a way that would be analogically repeatable, using just our own brains and a stopwatch.\" \"The next step in the transformation,\" said creature shop supervisor Jeff Mann, \"was to take the head off the first puppet - which at this point was in the ostrich position - and replace it with a hybrid head and neck that was something between the shrunken goat head and an actual ostrich head. We thought it was necessary to put that extra step in there mostly because of the color difference - the goat was white, while the ostrich was a dirty gray. Next came the actual ostrich puppet, which was also fully armatured. Its wings and legs moved and its beak was able to articulate a couple of lines of dialogue. This puppet looked just like a real ostrich - the transformation from goat was complete at this point - and it was about two feet tall. Four days before we were to shoot it, however, George called up and said that he would really like to have peacock feathers on the ostrich. Instead of machining an elaborate mechanism to make the peacock feathers fan out, Jeffrey Wilkins and Tad came up with the idea of using umbrella mechanisms, which worked just great. We put real peacock feathers on them and the effect was wonderful, though we found we had to work very quickly on stage because the heat from the lights made the feathers wilt in no time.\" Next came the turtle - a simple puppet with an expandable neck made of the same compressed foam as was used on the goat puppet. \"The turtle looked so real,\" said Krzanowski, \"that we were afraid someone would look at it and say, 'Gee, what did you do to that poor animal?' After a moment, the turtle transforms into the tiger.\" At the outset, the transformation from puppet turtle to live-action tiger seemed to be the most difficult to achieve. Once into the sculpting, however, Gordon Tate discovered some remarkable similarities in the two shapes. \"Gordon positioned the turtle shell as if it were the top of the tiger's head - and when we looked at it from a certain angle, the space between the turtle's legs created the eyes of the tiger in shadow. Then the legs pulled in and the turtle head - when it was tilted down - became the nose of the tiger. The turtle arms became the tiger arms coming out from underneath that little shell - and those too were made in that special foam and held with a cable. The whole thing was pretty weird-looking, but the idea was just to get us moving in the direction of the tiger and also give the computer guys something to go with. We had one more rod puppet between the turtle and the tiger - big knuckly tiger paws that had just a bit of movement - then the computer did the rest of the work to get it to the real tiger.\" To bridge the final transformation, a special suit - half-tiger and half-human - was fashioned to be worn by a stand-in actress. Ultimately, however, it was deleted in favor of an uninterrupted computer generated transition. It was the computer imagery that would give the sequential array of creature shop puppets a smooth flow from goat to woman. \"Our job,\" said computer graphics department cosupervisor Douglas Kay, \"was to take the puppet footage - which looked very realistic - and use digital image processing techniques to stretch or distort the characters further so that it would look as if each one was literally changing its shape and transforming into the next. For us, that meant five major steps. First the imagery had to be scanned into the computer. Then articulate mattes had to be made because each of the puppets had rods and wires attached to them. Next a bluescreen matte had to be extracted since the footage had been shot against a typical bluescreen and we wanted to deliver to optical both a color element and a matte element. The fourth step was the actual transformation which involved special software to distort an image from one shape to another. Finally it all had to be scanned back out onto film. Except for the last, all of these steps involved brand-new technology - either new hardware or new software. Digital images at full theatrical resolution require tremendous amounts of computer storage, so we had to come up with a method for storing all this data as well.\" The first step - scanning the film imagery - required the After the transformation puppets and live-action subjects were photographed on stage, the resulting bluescreen footage was turned over to the computer graphics department where it was scanned and digitized. In the digital mode, articulate mattes were produced to eliminate rods and other extraneous impediments. Then a bluescreen extraction was initiated to isolate color and matte elements. Computer team member Lincoln Hu operates a system keyboard while cosupervisor Douglas Kay studies a bluescreen tiger. / The transition from one form to another was accomplished by software engineer Doug Smythe. Working simultaneously with two digitized film scenes - one of the originating character and one of the terminating character - Smythe selected corresponding points on each and then developed a program that would digitally average the two. Done over a succession of frames, the resulting effect was a smooth transition from one discrete character to CINEFEX 35 • 59"],["60","Willow","Raziel invades Baumorda's possession of Cherlindrea's energy and springs suddenly to life - a puppet sequence employing traditional stoppotion meant for Elora and is enveloped in a cloud of toxic smoke. A late decision dematerializing required a last-minute mustering of Skeletal renderings preceded by interior skull glows were optically printed over the additional smoke elements were devised to replace her Succumbing to her own evil spell, Baumorda disappears through a ceiling grate in a Restored to human form, tower retreat for a final showdown with the evil queen. As the two sorceresses struggle for wand, a brazier is struck with a random blast of realized by Dave Allen motion animation. / Baumorda meets her end when she spills a deadly to include a closeup showing Baumorda animation resources. live-action features of actress Jean Marsh each time she was struck by animated lightning, and as she dissolved. / swirl of red smoke. 60 • CINEFEX 35 creation of a new input scanning system developed and operated by Scott Squires, Lincoln Hu and Les Dittert. \"The new scanner is based on a CCD (charge-coupled device) array,\" said Kay, \"which converts luminance levels in an image to electronic form. Development of the scanner required the integration by ILM of the CCD scanning unit, a film transport, a lamphouse, optics, a computer hardware interface, interface driver software and operations software. What was significant about this for us was that it was the first time we tried to scan in live-action footage, process it and scan it back out again without artifacts creeping in. Unlike previous projects where we had only a few frames to do or maybe an out-of-focus sequence or some highresolution still backgrounds, this was actual running footage that we were trying to scan and still have it look normal. We were dealing with a hundred or more frames of each of the different puppet creatures -all moving and showing either pain or some other emotion. So we would have one moving puppet at the start of the transformation and another moving puppet at the end - and at any particular point we were doing some transition between the two.\" Once the raw footage was scanned into the computer, articulate matte work was required to remove the rod mechanisms and cables employed on the stage. In the optical environment, rotoscoping would have been used to accomplish such a task; but in the computer realm, the same work can be accomplished on a video monitor with a digital painting system. Although the computer graphics department had such a system, it was inadequate to deal with the transformation workload. \"Our existing system,\" said software engineer Jonathan Luskin, \"did not have the ability to work with really large images - 'large' in the sense of data size. These images were something like 3500 pixels by 1400 pixels - which is a lot — so we needed a system that would allow us to work without actually being able to see every single dot on the screen at any particular time. That was one of the modifications I made - reducing the picture to something smaller so that we could see the whole thing on the monitor at once. We had to be able to pan around and approach different parts of the image without reducing it. We also put in an 'undo' button that let us reverse anything we had just done, which was very helpful and made us brave enough to attempt things we normally would not have tried.\" Making his digital debut on the newly modified system was animator Nick Stern. \"The painting system allowed me to select a color out of the background and replace it - which is the basic idea of all bluescreen work - but it took me a while to get used to it. At first I missed the flexibility of the animation rotostand - particularly the ability to go forward or backward through frames to review whatever it is you have done. Fortunately, Jon was able to develop a good program for looping frames which eliminated that concern. A lot of our problems were very similar to things we would encounter in roto, such as dealing with elements that had hair and uneven surfaces. For some reason, most of the transformation shots were done on a surface that resembled the coloring of the puppet - perhaps to help minimize any mistakes that occurred in our procedure - but that really made it more difficult for us, because often it was not obvious on every frame exactly where the foot ended and the floor began. One of the nice things about the system was the digital brush. That also took some getting used to, but it eliminated the painting step that we normally have to do in animation. With it we were able to do our articulate and paint at the same time - which saved a lot of time. Also the digital paint was layered, so we were able to rub through to a different layer and bring a certain color or detail through to the object we were painting on. A case in point was the tiger image. During the live-action shoot, the trainer used a metal chain that at one point was caught on film crossing the tiger's paw. By bringing up a previous clean frame of the tiger's paw, we were able to rub through that and get rid of the chain.\" Bluescreen extraction was the next step - taking elements against blue and separating out both a color element and a matte element that could be given to the optical department. \"The reason we did the extraction at this point,\" Kay explained, \"was that in each of these transformations the puppet was changing so dramatically and the backgrounds were changing so much that it would have been a fairly difficult bluescreen extraction problem to do afterwards because the conditions were changing for each and every one. It was easier to do it ahead of time to all of the elements.\" extraction into digital language was created by Jon Luskin and department cosupervisor George Joblove. \"We started out by sitting down with Bruce Nicholson and going over exactly what the steps are that are used in optical to pull a bluescreen matte,\" Joblove related. \"We broke the whole process down and then figured out how we would replicate each step digitally. It was a convenient way for us to go because then if things were not looking quite right or if Bruce suggested that we try something to improve the matte, we were able to talk in the same terms that he was using.\" In general, the computer graphics department encountered the same kinds of difficulties in bluescreen extraction that the optical department traditionally faces. \"The things that are simple for optical are simple for us. A pixel that is bright blue can easily be made black - assuming that the subject isn't wearing a bright blue top or something like that. The tricky part comes where things are halfway in between the subject and the blue - for instance around an edge or around fine blond hair or light tiger fur or where the subject is moving so that there is blur. That blur is a half-andhalf mixture of the yellowish subject and the bluish background which would then look gray, and we would have to figure out if that was a mixture of blue and yellow or if it actually was gray. There is no real solution - no algorithmic solution - to that kind of problem. We were just always trying to adjust things shot by shot.\" To help make those adjustments, Luskin gave the program"],["61","Willow","CINEFEX 35 • 61"],["62","Willow","the ability to process small sections of the images very quickly. \"We could tweak a parameter and then about one to two minutes later we would see the result. So we had a fairly fast iteration time. It wasn't like tweaking a knob on a video screen and seeing a real-time response, but it was certainly better than running something through an optical printer and waiting several hours to a day to see it on film.\" In other respects as well, the digital matting process actually proved to be simpler than traditional optical procedures. \"To do a composite digitally we didn't need both a clear-core matte and a black-core matte,\" Luskin revealed. \"We managed to get away with one matte. We started out with a very straight color-difference technique - similar to what the video Ultimatte system uses - and we ran into the same kinds of fringing problems and other things that happen when they try to do that optically. So then we used some of the more advanced methods employed in the optical department to create our mattes in other ways, but still retaining a synthetic blue record which we make with the colordifference technique. We did it differently for each shot. If you look at the ones we did in the beginning and compare them one me in the with the ones we did in the end, you would see that they got much better because we learned as we went along. We lack some of the tools for manipulating mattes that the optical people have, but the biggest area we are lacking in is experience. The optical department has so much experience, and we just haven't been at it that long.\" After the bluescreen extraction, the footage went to Doug Smythe for the actual transformation process. The object was to take each of two successive puppet characters and merge them into a single flowing image that at any given point represented a digital average of the two. \"During the transformation process,\" said Smythe, \"I worked with fairly low-resolution images that allowed me to fit one frame of film from each sequence on the screen at the same time, along with my menu of tools that I have to work with. Then on a frame-by-frame basis, I would pick corresponding points on the two images and the computer program would calculate where those points had to move to get from one to the other. For example, I would put dots around the eyes or along stripes on the two figures and the program would calculate how they needed to move towards each other to merge at the end. It was as if we had printed each frame of film on a rubber sheet and then used an array of pins that could be stuck into the rubber at various points and moved around - left and right or up and down.\" \"For each creature at the beginning of a transformation we have a strip of film that ends on a certain frame,\" Joblove elaborated, \"and then we have the first frame of the creature into which it is going to transform. Within each frame we establish a grid of dots that identify particular features and correlate point by point - at say the eyes or the ears or the mouth - between the beginning creature and the fully transformed one. We do that for the first frame and the last frame and for a number of key in-between frames as well. Then the program 62 * CINEFEX 35"],["63","Willow","will interpolate grid pairs for every pair of frames and create a new third frame in which the grid points are some sort of a weighted average between their position in the 'A' sequence and their position in the 'B' sequence. So in essence, a flow is established and the result is a geometric distortion of one shape into another shape - with eyes matching eyes and fingers matching fingers. At the same time, we do a dissolve that is cued to this grid so that we can also control different parts of the grid dissolving at different rates.\" \"All of our transformations were done at low-resolution first,\" said Smythe, \"to see if the motions were correct and if the different points were matching up properly. Once everything was right, the information would be given over to a separate program that would accomplish the same transformation with the full-resolution version overnight. There were some limitations to the overall technique. The biggest was that we could not have one portion of an image fold over another. The point with the lower numbered coordinate - like point four in row one - could not go to the right of point five. It had to stay strictly to the leit of that point. We could squish and stretch the grid, but we could not fold one piece over the other. So if one creature had a protruding chin but the other creature had a flat chin - or was looking in a slightly different direction so the chin could not be seen - then this problem would show up. We hope to make some program modifications to deal with this in the future; but given the constraints of production, we did not have time to work it out for Willow.\" Because they were dealing with hundreds of images at almost full film resolution, finding a means to store the magnitude of data was crucial to the success of the project. \"We were dealing with vast amounts of data,\" Joblove related, \"so our old kinds of computer tapes and storage discs just would not have worked. Since each frame represented five to ten megabytes and there are twenty-four frames per second, it is obvious why we needed a new storage system. Les Dittert investigated a number of alternatives, and we decided to go with a tape drive that uses standard 8mm videocassette tapes and gets more than two gigabytes on a single tape - which is roughly the capacity of a digital optical laserdisc. The advantage with the tapes is that they are considerably cheaper and the medium is widely available and easily handled. Also they are smaller than optical discs and less fragile. To give some sense of the capacity, personal computers typically come with around twenty megabytes of disc storage on them and one of these tapes holds one hundred times that amount. So it can store quite a phenomenal amount of data on one very small tape.\" The final step - output scanning - would be the only part of the process that did not involve some sort of new technology. \"We used the same laser film scanner that we have used on previous projects,\" said Joblove. \"Once all the pictures are computed for a sequence, they are transferred to the laser scanner's computer, either over the network or by reading them from 8mm tape. Frame by frame, each picture is read into a frame buffer, which then sends it pixel-by-pixel via a high-speed bus to the laser scanner as three channels of data - red, green and blue. This pixel data modulates red, green and blue laser beams, and is transmitted in synchronization with the oscillation of a mirror which reflects the beams so as to trace a raster pattern directly onto a Vistavision frame of 5247 negative film. At any instant, the beam is positioned at that portion of the image corresponding to the location of the pixel currently being read and the beam's color is that specified by the pixel values - so the film is exposed with an image of the picture in the frame buffer. All this is done at full resolution - about 3500 pixels across - and takes about a minute per frame. Since we have already extracted the elements from the bluescreen, we are able to scan out onto film the transformed element against black and its corresponding matte. Those two elements are then passed on to the optical department where the matte we have generated is used to composite our foreground transformation against whatever background element is involved in the scene.i Including software development time, the computer graphics department devoted five-and-a-half months to the transformation project. Willow and his band have a powerful ally in Raziel once she is finally restored to her human form. With the assault on Nockmaar by Madmartigan and his rebel alliance, Raziel confronts Bavmorda in the castle tower for a final showdown of sorcerous power. At first, Bavmorda gains the upper hand, tossing a fireball at Raziel which momentarily engulfs her in a maelstrom of flame. \"Using gas jets on stage, Bob Finley - our pyrotechnician - was able to create a controlled vortex of fire,\" said Wes Takahashi, \"which was then printed on top of Raziel. To make it appear as if it were encircling her, Chris Green created some animated fire edge mattes that had the same nature as the fire itself. Then to make the effect even more convincing, an animated interactive light element was printed through the interpositive of Raziel to imply a textured reflective light from the flames.\" As the two formidable rivals grapple for possession of Cherlindrea's magic wand, blasts of lightning-like energy are emitted from it, haphazardly endowing inanimate objects with life. Animator Gordon Baker was responsible for producing the blasts, which were timed to coincide with explosive squibs detonated on the set during principal photography. \"Gordon fashioned the basic energy bolts from backlit artwork with ripple glass over it. After that he did another pass to create hot spots that ran throughout the wand blasts. All of that was comped with the plate in optical where some further diffusion and a blue color were added to it, along with some vaseline to enhance the hot spots even more.\" In the final film, only a brazier is jolted to life by the random blasts of magical energy. Originally, however, the sequence was also to have featured a walking chair and a living gargoyle. Rod puppetry and stop-motion animation were to be used, and David Allen was engaged to do the work. \"Models for the gargoyle and the chair were built by Phil Tippett's crew,\" Allen recalled. Though practical smoke effects were employed on stage during Baumorda's final scene, the animation department was engaged to supplement and give cohesive form to the deadly swirling cloud. Working with technical designer Bruce Walters, animator John Armstrong devised an innovative slit-streak technique to create and control a volume of smoke via light streaks photographed through ripple glass. / Throughout the production, the animation department crossed the boundaries of traditional celbound animation. Tasked with providing wing imagery for dozens of fluttering fairies, department head Wes Takahashi constructed miniature wings from etched plastic which were then mounted on a custom-built motion control flapping rig. Photographed full-frame, the wings were then pin-blocked and composited - with extensive articulate rotomatting - onto footage of the live performers. CINEFEX 35 • 63"],["64","Willow","With Baumorda destroyed returns home in triumph. His joyous reunion with originally to have ended with a simple pull-back on the Nelwyn village, During postproduction, however, it was decided that something more dramatic was in order - especially since the final screen while the end credits rolled. The matte department responded with a majestic shot - designed by Chris Evans - that began in tight pulled back to reveal the and Elora saved, Willow family and friends was image would remain on on the village and then entire Nelwyn valley. 64 * CINEFEX 35 \"Craig Davies designed an especially interesting approach to the gargoyle. The idea was that it really was a rock sculpture come to life - not a rock turning into something else. So everything was stiff and blocky and the armature was built to move on the centers of all these rigid parts. It was like a sleeved and jointed thing rather than a flexible rubber thing. I thought it was really clever and I would like to have seen that model finished off. The chair was finished off - and photographed. It was a rod puppet, and Bob Cooper and I shot over a hundred takes during a three-day period to finally get one that had the subtle features everybody was hoping to see. We shot it bluescreen and we also shot the background for it - a miniature set of the tower. It was all ready to go to optical when we got the word that both the chair and the gargoyle were being cut. I guess for timing or pacing reasons it was necessary; but I thought it was unfortunate that the chair, especially, was cut because it was a good setup for the brazier coming to life later.\" Struck by a chance bolt from the magic wand, the brazierdubbed the 'Webersaurus' because of its resemblance to a kettle-style barbeque - flies up into the air and then comes down and charges Willow who grabs a pitchfork to defend himself. A stop-motion sparing match and tug-of-war ensues until Willow at last manages to overturn the brazier and slide it out through a window. \"We shot the sequence bluescreen and it was done almost entirely without go-motion, which is unusual for ILM. Because of the design of the model, we couldn't loosen the joints in the ankles; and since go-motion depends on being able to tighten and loosen certain joints on demand in order for the model mover to carry the puppet without resistance, I had no option but to animate the brazier the old-fashioned way. There were some model mover assisters on the thing which allowed me to do some of the more severe moves - like when it first flies up in the air and later when it flips over. Terry Chostner was the cameraman and he helped set those up, along with his assistant Bob Hill. Since we were shooting the model against bluescreen, the entire animation process needed to take place on risers - stand-off spacers - so that the legs could be lifted up off of the actual animation deck and still allow the entire model to be surrounded by blue. I used to be somewhat putoff by having to animate this way - it's like animating in space, which can be aesthetically disorienting - but there are advantages to having spacers underneath the feet. Because the puppet was four or five inches up in the air all the time, by removing the spacer I could actually get under the foot for better leverage - which I would not have been able to do if it were right on the table. It was rather free-style animation.\" Though for the most part motion control was not employed to motivate the puppet, it was a factor in duplicating camera moves present in the live-action plate photography. \"Most of the camera moves were not really dramatic - just sort of floating and loose. The first thing we had to do was plot the move on the plate so that our camera would be doing substantially what the plate camera was doing each time we shot a frame. Terry and I both programmed that into the motion control. Once that was done, we no longer had to worry about it when we were actually animating. Doing the lineups for the tug-ofwar was a problem, though, because at ILM they do not have any way of loading in sequentially repeating frames of a background plate that can be superimposed over the taking camera lens. In other words, I would not have been able to take the frames of Willow maneuvering the pitchfork and line each one up with the brazier through the viewfinder. In order to do that, your animation camera must be custom machined with retractable pins in the bore-sight door that will allow you to lift the frame out and go to the next one. Then by racking over you can see what that frame is doing relative to where you are. With the present cameras at ILM there is just no way of doing that. In fact, there is not even a way of putting a clip in or taking one out once the camera is boxed up for shooting - you have to do all the lineup beforehand. I mentioned this to Dennis and all that they he said that they had never had to deal with that before. which I was very surprised to hear. So the best thing we could come up with was to take an easel board with animation pegs on it and cut a window through it. Once that was done, the easel was suspended between the puppet and the camera. By carefully tracing each live-action frame on a Moviola, we were able to mount those tracings on the easel. Then by removing the ground glass from the viewing tube to produce an aerial image, we could look through them all the way back to the puppet. It was not as good as a clip since we had introduced an element of interpretation in the outlining, but it was the best we could do under the circumstances. That particular shot was about five seconds long and we had to plot it for each frame.\" A contribution of the optical department to the overall chaos of the sequence was the introduction of rain, frost and snow into the tower - all elements of the stormlike conditions produced by the uncontrolled wand. \"Since the wand was jiggling and waving around,\" said optical cosupervisor John Ellis, \"it was very unsteady, yet the frost and snow had to come out of the end of it. The snow was shot on the stage and there was no attempt to match the move - they just had a locked-off nozzle. So we had to match the frost element and the snow coming out of the end of the wand on the optical camera. We have a motion control printer now, so we can match moves on elements without having to dupe them later on. As a result, it is fairly easy for us to plot moves on the printer rather than take footage back to an animation stand or have to move it by hand on one of the optical cameras. John Alexander plotted the moves, doing only certain key frames as in animation. Then the computer wou thin the would fill in the gaps. After that we would look at it to see if everything was working. If it was not, we would plot some more frames and try again.\" While Raziel and Bavmorda continue to clash, Willow makes his way to the baby Elora and attempts to spirit her out of the tower. Bavmorda temporarily incapacitates her opponent, however, and thwarts their escape. Reaching into his pocket for a"],["65","Willow","CINEFEX 35 • 65"],["66","Willow","66 - CINEFEX 35"],["67","Willow","magic acorn that is supposed to turn living things to stone, Willow tosses the tiny charm at Bavmorda who catches it in her fist. Suddenly her hand and arm begin to stiffen. \"Bavmorda's hand turning to rock was achieved by isolating her hand and forearm in the live-action frame and wiping over them a sort of fractured animation element,\" Dennis Muren explained. \"Over the area of the wipe was a plotted bipack element of a stonelike surface. So what we had was the hand in black-and-white and beneath it a surface of stone visible through animated cracks in this fracturing formation.\" Although taken aback by Willow's audacity, Bavmorda is too powerful for the trivial acor magic and promptly reverses the spell. In the end, Bavmorda's undoing comes in the form of a deadly liquid intended for the destruction of the baby princess. Knocking over some bowls containing the concoction, Bavmorda unleashes a spidery swirl of smoke that promptly sweeps over her. \"Dave Carson was responsible for the initial look of the sequence,\" noted Wes Takahashi, \"which called for dense red smoke to envelop Bavmorda and whisk her away to some servo. The netherworld. The sequence starts off with a real smoke element that was shot on the stage. It was red-colored and just spread out on the floor. We were supposed to take it from there, but nobody knew exactly how we were going to do it. John Armstrong had come up with a smoke effect for The Golden Child that was never used and so he decided to apply it here. Basically it involved a distorted slit-streak technique. Bruce Walters acted in the capacity of technical advisor responsible for programming the camera moves from John's layouts and for building the intricate apparatus to accomplish the effect. Together they were able to create a mysterious ethereal smoke that twisted around Bavmorda's body and eventually rose up and through the ceiling grate. As the sequence began, roto added a glow to the cups of liquid that were eventually knocked over by the evil queen. Stage smoke was then shot and comped in to appear as if it were rising up out of the mixed liquid. From there the animated smoke continued the action of encircling her body. Once the effect was completed, it was up to John Alexander - using the news motion control printer - to position the smoke into the tilting background. Bavmorda also gets hit by lightning while this is going on so John Armstrong animated a kind of static electricity that runs through the smoke itself. Finally Sean Turner came up with a body effect which had the intensity of electricity, but was a lot more organic in nature - almost like a cellular web that's enveloping her body and just coming on and off sporadically.\" With only a week-and-a-half left before the production deadline - and with most reels of the film already locked and in the process of being printed for release - it was decided that a final closeup was needed to give the impression that Bavmorda was somehow dematerializing. \"With no holds barred and throwing in everything but the kitchen sink,\" said Takahashi, \"Tim Berglund created some nice skeletal renderings that during the optical process were printed through the interpositive of Bavmorda every time she was struck by lightning. John Armstrong created interior skull glows to precede each skeletal image and also worked with Bruce Walters on a smoke element that would match the other shots. Charlie Clavadetscher and I came up with an image of smoke that would replace Bavmorda as she dissolved. With barely one chance at comping these elements together, it was up to Bruce Vecchitto in optical lineup and John Alexander to make the educated guesses needed to put the shot together without even the benefit of wedges. Miraculously, everything worked.\" Tracking each and every shot from storyboard to final composite were effects editors Michael Gleason, Michael Moore and Steven R. Moore. Like everyone else on the ILM team, they were overwhelmed with the workload - but not surprised by it. \"From the beginning of this show,\" Gleason recalled, \"George's edict was, 'This is not an effects picture.' But when the storyboards started coming in, we all thought to ourselves, 'Oh, really?' The real challenge editorially was just coping with the volume of film - beginning with approximately seven hundred and fifty background plates shot on locations all over the world. Because of that volume, there was a lot of pressure to get everything right the first time. Our final count was about four hundred effects shots - which for a picture that wasn't supposed to be an effects picture was sure an awful lot of effects.\" In many ways, the shoot-from-the-hip approach to visual operate effects as practiced on Willow made the project the antithesis of what ILM usually demands when working as an independent contractor. In fact, ILM's biggest in-house production in years seems to have afforded its resident artists an opportunity to throw out most of their own rules with regard to meticulous preplanning. Had Willow been anything but a Lucasfilm production, ILM might well have turned the project down as unmanageable. \"But this was for the boss,\" Michael McAlister concluded, \"and the boss gets to work however he wants. On this particular project, the looser approach made sense because it was such an enormous undertaking. If we had taken lots of preproduction time to plan and then production time to execute shots in an exact way, a lot of that time would have been wasted on shots that did not end up in the movie. The feeling was why spend a lot of time on all the shots when we can spend a reasonable amount of time on them and then worry about any nem and then we know exactly mistakes we might make at the back end when we know exactly what shots are going to be in the film? It probably is cheaper in the long run to throw money at the shots here at ILM than it is to spend too much time on location with the clock ticking by at thousands of dollars a second. There is no doubt that the approach limited what we were able to do - if there had been more preparation we would have been better able to utilize all our talents and tricks. But I think we all appreciated George's point of view in terms of getting the movie shot and trying to do the effects as though they were a part of normal production. It was an ambitious approach - and it worked. A balance was struck there somewhere.\" Chris Evans adds finishing touches to the expansive final painting. / On an elevated platform erected to achieve a proper viewing angle, the matte department camera crew - headed by Craig Barron - prepares to shoot a live-action plate for the shot. Photographed in an open field behind the ILM complex, the plate featured only a handful of costumed extras and a few minor props and set pieces. Also incorporated into the shot was a separately photographed river plate. Everything else - including most of the huts - was in the artwork. / Matte artist Michael Pangrazio completes a painting of the castle at Tir Asleen. Composited into the finished shot were several groups of bluescreened wellwishers and a pair of miniature towers complete with banners and flags. CINEFEX 35 • 67"],["68","Closing pages",""]]});
